[Event "Chapter 1: Destroying e3 Lines / Study 1: Fighting 5.e3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/d9de72f7-728d-4cee-8bef-6808bb86bed3/studies/3d93ef20-4820-44ca-94db-8c4fffdf4450/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the course on the Albin Countergambit! This is a fun and powerful weapon against the Queen's gambit that may come as a big surprise to a lot of White players, especially those who mostly prepare with main lines. It's characterized by ...e5 on move 2, potentially leading to extremely sharp and tactical lines that can easily pose problems for White. White opens with d4, and we'll reply with d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We will play ...e5, the Albin Countergambit! We attack the center with another pawn and sacrifice the pawn on e5 for space and rapid development.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {If White wants to prove this gambit is dubious, they have to accept it. And they will more than half the time, in 3.3 million games. Our idea is to push the pawn to d4! This pawn is protected and already restricts White's standard developing move: Nc3. Apart from that, the e5-pawn and the c4-pawn are now split and the e5-pawn is already in our territory and vulnerable.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {Study 1 will deal with Nf3 on move 4. White wants to attack the pawn first and then play e3 to challenge it. This is played 70% of the time, far more frequently than 4.e3 which we will look in the next study. There's only one natural move to protect the pawn, and that's ...Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.e3 {White challenges the pawn now and opens up the bishop. This move is the most popular, and occurs in 25% of games in the database. They have to play this move at some point to continue development, so you can expect it. We'll give a check on b4. This is a critical move and the gambit cannot work without it. White can't block on c3 thanks to our d4-pawn and will therefore have to block on d2.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Bd2 {White will most likely block with the bishop here. Don't capture the bishop as a reflex. The key move is taking the pawn on e3. White's bishop is pinned for the moment so they can't recapture with it. And both the bishop and the f2-pawn are under attack.} 6...dxe3 7.fxe3 {If White wants to recapture on e3, they have to do so with the pawn. Now, having damaged White's pawns, we are happy to exchange on d2.} 7...Bxd2+ 8.Qxd2 {The queen recaptures. The standard idea for Black in these variations of the Albin is playing against the weak e-pawns, and you have to keep this in mind at all times. This is why we unleashed the ...Bb4+ and ...dxe3 maneuver in the first place. Now the bishop activation to g4 targets the defender of the e5-pawn.} 8...Bg4 9.Be2 {White defends. We shouldn't rush and give up the bishop on f3 yet. We can develop instead: a common idea is bringing our knight to g6 and putting more pressure on the e5-pawn.} 9...Nge7 10.O-O {White could choose to castle here. Having activated both knights, we can comfortably trade the queens.} 10...Qxd2 11.Nbxd2 {White recaptures. We are fine with the queen trade. because we can now castle long and keep the momentum. Apart from our knight arriving at g6, the h-rook is also coming to the center.} 11...O-O-O 12.h3 {White could nonchalantly play h3, thinking they simply attack your bishop. But there's a tactic after this move. We can capture on d2! The white bishop is also under attack.} 12...Rxd2 13.Nxd2 {White accepts the temporary sacrifice... But now we get two pieces for the rook. Nine times out of ten, this is worth it.} 13...Bxe2 14.Rxf7 {Even at the price of dropping another pawn.} 14...Rg8 {Let's protect the g7-pawn. White is left with all the weaknesses they had before, but fewer pieces to defend with. Black is much better.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Destroying e3 Lines / Study 1: Fighting 5.e3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/d9de72f7-728d-4cee-8bef-6808bb86bed3/studies/3d93ef20-4820-44ca-94db-8c4fffdf4450/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the course on the Albin Countergambit! This is a fun and powerful weapon against the Queen's gambit that may come as a big surprise to a lot of White players, especially those who mostly prepare with main lines. It's characterized by ...e5 on move 2, potentially leading to extremely sharp and tactical lines that can easily pose problems for White. White opens with d4, and we'll reply with d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We will play ...e5, the Albin Countergambit! We attack the center with another pawn and sacrifice the pawn on e5 for space and rapid development.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {If White wants to prove this gambit is dubious, they have to accept it. And they will more than half the time, in 3.3 million games. Our idea is to push the pawn to d4! This pawn is protected and already restricts White's standard developing move: Nc3. Apart from that, the e5-pawn and the c4-pawn are now split and the e5-pawn is already in our territory and vulnerable.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {Study 1 will deal with Nf3 on move 4. White wants to attack the pawn first and then play e3 to challenge it. This is played 70% of the time, far more frequently than 4.e3 which we will look in the next study. There's only one natural move to protect the pawn, and that's ...Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.e3 {White challenges the pawn now and opens up the bishop. This move is the most popular, and occurs in 25% of games in the database. They have to play this move at some point to continue development, so you can expect it. We'll give a check on b4. This is a critical move and the gambit cannot work without it. White can't block on c3 thanks to our d4-pawn and will therefore have to block on d2.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Bd2 {White will most likely block with the bishop here. Don't capture the bishop as a reflex. The key move is taking the pawn on e3. White's bishop is pinned for the moment so they can't recapture with it. And both the bishop and the f2-pawn are under attack.} 6...dxe3 7.fxe3 {If White wants to recapture on e3, they have to do so with the pawn. Now, having damaged White's pawns, we are happy to exchange on d2.} 7...Bxd2+ 8.Qxd2 {The queen recaptures. The standard idea for Black in these variations of the Albin is playing against the weak e-pawns, and you have to keep this in mind at all times. This is why we unleashed the ...Bb4+ and ...dxe3 maneuver in the first place. Now the bishop activation to g4 targets the defender of the e5-pawn.} 8...Bg4 9.Qxd8+ {White can also trade queens here. We'll recapture with the rook. This only helps our activity.} 9...Rxd8 10.Nbd2 {The knight comes out to e2 now, supporting the f3-knight. We will still get the e5-pawn, though. Because we can add an attacker to it with Nge7-g6. And White is all out of defenders.} 10...Nge7 11.h3 {We don't even have to take on f3 if White attacks the bishop.} 11...Be6 {Retreating to e6 and keeping the bishop is good because we also have to play on the light squares. Next, our knight is coming to g6 and the e5-pawn will soon die.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Destroying e3 Lines / Study 1: Fighting 5.e3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/d9de72f7-728d-4cee-8bef-6808bb86bed3/studies/3d93ef20-4820-44ca-94db-8c4fffdf4450/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the course on the Albin Countergambit! This is a fun and powerful weapon against the Queen's gambit that may come as a big surprise to a lot of White players, especially those who mostly prepare with main lines. It's characterized by ...e5 on move 2, potentially leading to extremely sharp and tactical lines that can easily pose problems for White. White opens with d4, and we'll reply with d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We will play ...e5, the Albin Countergambit! We attack the center with another pawn and sacrifice the pawn on e5 for space and rapid development.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {If White wants to prove this gambit is dubious, they have to accept it. And they will more than half the time, in 3.3 million games. Our idea is to push the pawn to d4! This pawn is protected and already restricts White's standard developing move: Nc3. Apart from that, the e5-pawn and the c4-pawn are now split and the e5-pawn is already in our territory and vulnerable.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {Study 1 will deal with Nf3 on move 4. White wants to attack the pawn first and then play e3 to challenge it. This is played 70% of the time, far more frequently than 4.e3 which we will look in the next study. There's only one natural move to protect the pawn, and that's ...Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.e3 {White challenges the pawn now and opens up the bishop. This move is the most popular, and occurs in 25% of games in the database. They have to play this move at some point to continue development, so you can expect it. We'll give a check on b4. This is a critical move and the gambit cannot work without it. White can't block on c3 thanks to our d4-pawn and will therefore have to block on d2.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Bd2 {White will most likely block with the bishop here. Don't capture the bishop as a reflex. The key move is taking the pawn on e3. White's bishop is pinned for the moment so they can't recapture with it. And both the bishop and the f2-pawn are under attack.} 6...dxe3 7.Bxb4 {Taking the bishop on b4 instead of the pawn doesn't work. White's move allows the capture on f2 with check.} 7...exf2+ 8.Ke2 {The king has to slide to e2 to keep an eye on the queen. We'll trade now.} 8...Qxd1+ 9.Kxd1 {The king has to move again. Finally, we'll recapture the bishop. We went from being a pawn down to being a pawn up, and White has lost their castling rights. Their e5-pawn is also isolated and weak. They will get the f2-pawn back, but we will develop and castle in the meantime.} 9...Nxb4 10.a3 {White can attack our knight... But we'll simply move to c6 and attack the pawn.} 10...Nc6 11.Ke2 {The king goes after the pawn. It's great to see White making so many moves with the king and wasting time. Meanwhile, we will develop. There is no need to rush with ...Bg4 and ...Nxe5. The e5-pawn isn't going anywhere and castling is priority now.} 11...Bf5 12.Kxf2 {White captures on f2.} 12...O-O-O {And we will castle, activating the rook as well. Next, we will slowly build up our position until we eventually capture the e5-pawn. Black is much better.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Destroying e3 Lines / Study 1: Fighting 5.e3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/d9de72f7-728d-4cee-8bef-6808bb86bed3/studies/3d93ef20-4820-44ca-94db-8c4fffdf4450/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the course on the Albin Countergambit! This is a fun and powerful weapon against the Queen's gambit that may come as a big surprise to a lot of White players, especially those who mostly prepare with main lines. It's characterized by ...e5 on move 2, potentially leading to extremely sharp and tactical lines that can easily pose problems for White. White opens with d4, and we'll reply with d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We will play ...e5, the Albin Countergambit! We attack the center with another pawn and sacrifice the pawn on e5 for space and rapid development.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {If White wants to prove this gambit is dubious, they have to accept it. And they will more than half the time, in 3.3 million games. Our idea is to push the pawn to d4! This pawn is protected and already restricts White's standard developing move: Nc3. Apart from that, the e5-pawn and the c4-pawn are now split and the e5-pawn is already in our territory and vulnerable.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {Study 1 will deal with Nf3 on move 4. White wants to attack the pawn first and then play e3 to challenge it. This is played 70% of the time, far more frequently than 4.e3 which we will look in the next study. There's only one natural move to protect the pawn, and that's ...Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.e3 {White challenges the pawn now and opens up the bishop. This move is the most popular, and occurs in 25% of games in the database. They have to play this move at some point to continue development, so you can expect it. We'll give a check on b4. This is a critical move and the gambit cannot work without it. White can't block on c3 thanks to our d4-pawn and will therefore have to block on d2.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nbd2 {Blocking with the knight is less confrontational than blocking with the bishop, but it doesn't change our plans. We will capture the pawn on e3.} 6...dxe3 7.fxe3 {White has no choice but to recapture, and look at their pawns now. We are still down a pawn, but the doubled pawns on the e-file are simply waiting to be captured. The knight can jump to both g4 and f5 from the h6-square, and we can include the queen on e7 if we need to.} 7...Nh6 8.a3 {When White attacks our bishop, we don't have to trade for the knight. Bringing it back is fine. White's weak pawns are on the dark squares so our bishop can only help.} 8...Be7 9.Be2 {White continues development. They don't really have many ways of stopping our venture against the pawns. We can fork both pawns now with Ng4. White will surely lose one of them.} 9...Ng4 10.Ne4 {They can protect the e3-pawn by centralizing the knight, and offer a queen trade. But even a queenless middlegame works in our favor.} 10...Qxd1+ 11.Bxd1 {The bishop recaptures and White keeps castling rights.} 11...Ngxe5 {But the e5-pawn falls, and that's not the end of it. The e3-pawn is still extremely weak and we haven't forgotten about it! We will play against it next, already being better.} *

