[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/d9de72f7-728d-4cee-8bef-6808bb86bed3/studies/3d93ef20-4820-44ca-94db-8c4fffdf4450/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the course on the Albin Countergambit! This is a fun and powerful weapon against the Queen's gambit that may come as a big surprise to a lot of White players, especially those who mostly prepare with main lines. It's characterized by ...e5 on move 2, potentially leading to extremely sharp and tactical lines that can easily pose problems for White. White opens with d4, and we'll reply with d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We will play ...e5, the Albin Countergambit! We attack the center with another pawn and sacrifice the pawn on e5 for space and rapid development.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {If White wants to prove this gambit is dubious, they have to accept it. And they will more than half the time, in 3.3 million games. Our idea is to push the pawn to d4! This pawn is protected and already restricts White's standard developing move: Nc3. Apart from that, the e5-pawn and the c4-pawn are now split and the e5-pawn is already in our territory and vulnerable.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {Study 1 will deal with Nf3 on move 4. White wants to attack the pawn first and then play e3 to challenge it. This is played 70% of the time, far more frequently than 4.e3 which we will look in the next study. There's only one natural move to protect the pawn, and that's ...Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.e3 {White challenges the pawn now and opens up the bishop. This move is the most popular, and occurs in 25% of games in the database. They have to play this move at some point to continue development, so you can expect it. We'll give a check on b4. This is a critical move and the gambit cannot work without it. White can't block on c3 thanks to our d4-pawn and will therefore have to block on d2.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Bd2 {White will most likely block with the bishop here. Don't capture the bishop as a reflex. The key move is taking the pawn on e3. White's bishop is pinned for the moment so they can't recapture with it. And both the bishop and the f2-pawn are under attack.} 6...dxe3 7.fxe3 {If White wants to recapture on e3, they have to do so with the pawn. Now, having damaged White's pawns, we are happy to exchange on d2.} 7...Bxd2+ 8.Nbxd2 {If the knight recaptures on d2...} 8...Qe7 {We can simply bring the queen to e7 and White can't save the e5-pawn. We'll regain the material and maintain a much better pawn structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Destroying e3 Lines / Study 2: The 4.e3 Trap  / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/d9de72f7-728d-4cee-8bef-6808bb86bed3/studies/d0d9ca24-fc88-4273-82c9-18b324aada85/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 of the first chapter will show us how to approach the position when White is impatient and plays the dubious 4.e3. This premature move could easily be punished if White is not accurate, and in extremely entertaining ways at that! Let's go, starting with White's 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. We'll respond with the Albin Countergambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit. And it's important to push ...d4 here.} 3...d4 4.e3 {The move e3 here could be considered a mistake, and it's played 12% of the time in more than 330,000 games! It only goes to show that many people aren't prepared for the Albin. As in the last study, we will give this check. It will prove to be even more powerful now.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Nd2 {You probably already know what to do if White blocks with the knight. And that's to capture on e3.} 5...dxe3 6.fxe3 {White has to recapture, but this provokes other means of attack by us. As in the main line, once we capture on e3 the h4-e1 diagonal opens up. And we'll play Qh4+!} 6...Qh4+ 7.g3 {White will block with g3... And we can slide the queen to e4. Of course, it can't be captured by the knight because it's pinned. Look at our active queen attacking the rook and both vulnerable pawns at the same time!} 7...Qe4 8.Nf3 {Once White develops and blocks the attack on the rook, we don't even have to rush capturing either pawn. There is nothing White can do to defend them. We can take a move here to develop the knight with Nc6 and put more pressure on the e5-pawn.} 8...Nc6 9.Bg2 {It's best for White to develop and threaten a discovered attack on our queen. We'll capture on e3 now, with check.} 9...Qxe3+ 10.Qe2 {White blocks. Our idea is to trade queens.} 10...Qxe2+ 11.Kxe2 {After the king recaptures, White has lost castling rights. And that's not all.} 11...Be6 {White has even more weaknesses now, and the material is equal. We'll develop the bishop and target the c4-pawn. Apart from that, we can comfortably castle next move and bring the knight into the game. We're better.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Destroying e3 Lines / Study 2: The 4.e3 Trap  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/d9de72f7-728d-4cee-8bef-6808bb86bed3/studies/d0d9ca24-fc88-4273-82c9-18b324aada85/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 of the first chapter will show us how to approach the position when White is impatient and plays the dubious 4.e3. This premature move could easily be punished if White is not accurate, and in extremely entertaining ways at that! Let's go, starting with White's 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. We'll respond with the Albin Countergambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit. And it's important to push ...d4 here.} 3...d4 4.e3 {The move e3 here could be considered a mistake, and it's played 12% of the time in more than 330,000 games! It only goes to show that many people aren't prepared for the Albin. As in the last study, we will give this check. It will prove to be even more powerful now.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Bd2 {The critical line is White blocking with the bishop, of course. We haven't played ...Nc6 this time, so our bishop isn't protected at all. But we will still capture on e3!} 5...dxe3 6.fxe3 {The only way for White to maintain some kind of equality is to recapture on e3. But even then will we grab initiative. The queen gives check! The dark diagonal has opened up and there is no knight on f3 to cover the h4-square.} 6...Qh4+ 7.g3 {White blocks with the pawn, but we already know where our queen is going. And that's the e4-square to attack the rook and the e3- and e5-pawns.} 7...Qe4 8.Nf3 {White defends both with Nf3. But we can trade on d2 now.} 8...Bxd2+ 9.Nbxd2 {The knight has to recapture. And we can win the pawn back, with check.} 9...Qxe3+ 10.Qe2 {White blocks with the queen. Now trading is best.} 10...Qxe2+ 11.Bxe2 {The bishop recaptures. And now it's time to continue development.} 11...Nc6 {We'll start doing that with ...Nc6. As you already know, a common motif is bringing the other knight to g6 via e7 and harassing the e5-pawn. As far as the light-squared bishop is concerned, we can both activate it to e6 or to h3 to pose some problems on the light squares. It's more natural to castle long here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Destroying e3 Lines / Study 2: The 4.e3 Trap  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/d9de72f7-728d-4cee-8bef-6808bb86bed3/studies/d0d9ca24-fc88-4273-82c9-18b324aada85/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 of the first chapter will show us how to approach the position when White is impatient and plays the dubious 4.e3. This premature move could easily be punished if White is not accurate, and in extremely entertaining ways at that! Let's go, starting with White's 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. We'll respond with the Albin Countergambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit. And it's important to push ...d4 here.} 3...d4 4.e3 {The move e3 here could be considered a mistake, and it's played 12% of the time in more than 330,000 games! It only goes to show that many people aren't prepared for the Albin. As in the last study, we will give this check. It will prove to be even more powerful now.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Bd2 {The critical line is White blocking with the bishop, of course. We haven't played ...Nc6 this time, so our bishop isn't protected at all. But we will still capture on e3!} 5...dxe3 6.Bxb4 {If White takes the hanging bishop, they are completely lost. The reason is the following. We will capture on f2 with a fork and the king mustn't recapture because of the hanging queen on d1.} 6...exf2+ 7.Ke2 {So White has to slide to e2. Now if we take the knight and promote to a queen, White will be able to trade on d8 and then capture on g1. We would regain the piece but the position would be equal. Do you see something better? We can take on g1 and promote to...a knight! This is crucial because it comes with check and White doesn't have time to take on d8.} 7...fxg1=N+ 8.Rxg1 {White captures the knight, and we've gotten the piece back. But that's not the point.} 8...Bg4+ {The point is that we have this check! We will win the queen on the next move and the game is over.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Destroying e3 Lines / Study 2: The 4.e3 Trap  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/d9de72f7-728d-4cee-8bef-6808bb86bed3/studies/d0d9ca24-fc88-4273-82c9-18b324aada85/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 of the first chapter will show us how to approach the position when White is impatient and plays the dubious 4.e3. This premature move could easily be punished if White is not accurate, and in extremely entertaining ways at that! Let's go, starting with White's 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. We'll respond with the Albin Countergambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit. And it's important to push ...d4 here.} 3...d4 4.e3 {The move e3 here could be considered a mistake, and it's played 12% of the time in more than 330,000 games! It only goes to show that many people aren't prepared for the Albin. As in the last study, we will give this check. It will prove to be even more powerful now.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Bd2 {The critical line is White blocking with the bishop, of course. We haven't played ...Nc6 this time, so our bishop isn't protected at all. But we will still capture on e3!} 5...dxe3 6.Bxb4 {If White takes the hanging bishop, they are completely lost. The reason is the following. We will capture on f2 with a fork and the king mustn't recapture because of the hanging queen on d1.} 6...exf2+ 7.Ke2 {So White has to slide to e2. Now if we take the knight and promote to a queen, White will be able to trade on d8 and then capture on g1. We would regain the piece but the position would be equal. Do you see something better? We can take on g1 and promote to...a knight! This is crucial because it comes with check and White doesn't have time to take on d8.} 7...fxg1=N+ 8.Ke1 {Moving the king back to e1 doesn't help White's cause.} 8...Qh4+ {We can bring the queen out to h4 with check. White will have to move to d2 and they are simply busted.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.e3  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/a943b1dd-02ba-4622-be8d-d6786b8dee56/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 1 we saw that White can easily get in trouble if they aren't ready for our Albin Countergambit. If we manage to push ...d4 and give a check on b4 we could already be in good shape. However, when White knows what they're doing, they will prevent ...Bb4+ with a3 on move 5. And that is what Chapter 2 is all about. Let's begin, with 1.d4 for White and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts. And we'll push the pawn to d4. We already know that we want to give a check on b4 if White goes 4.Nf3 and 5.e3.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White plays Nf3 first. We'll defend the pawn. Now ...Bb4+ is an idea.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {Which is why the most annoying line from White is 5.a3, played just over 20% of the time, in over 330,000 games. White stops Bb4+, forcing us to change our plans. So we'll stop any potential White b4 advance by playing a5. Then we'll bring our bishop to c5, supporting d4. Apart from that, our ...Nge7 and ...Ng6 idea to target the e5-pawn is still viable. Other ideas to mention here for Black are 5...f6 to develop rapidly, 5...Bg4, planning ...Qe7 and ...O-O-O, and 5...Nge7 to go for the e5-pawn.} 5...a5 6.e3 {In this study, White challenges the center with e3 and opens up the bishop. This move is much better now that they have a pawn on a3. It's played 40% of the time. As mentioned, we'll bring the bishop to support the center.} 6...Bc5 7.exd4 {Expect to see this trade as White wants to get rid of our problematic d4-pawn. We'll recapture with the bishop, attacking the e5-pawn.} 7...Bxd4 8.Nxd4 {White chooses to capture again. We'll take with the queen, maintaining the attack on the e5-pawn. White could easily go wrong here.} 8...Qxd4 9.Nc3 {A better move than trading the queens is simply activating the knight to c3. We'll trade queens anyway. The d4-square being available for the knight is too tempting.} 9...Qxd1+ 10.Nxd1 {White recaptures with the knight. It's time to start targeting stuff. The e5-pawn is hanging, and we'll attack the c4-pawn twice if we capture it. On the other hand, the knight jumping to d4 and threatening a fork is also potent.} 10...Be6 11.Bf4 {White can defend the e5-pawn for now.} 11...O-O-O {But we'll castle with the same ideas, with the added ...Nge7 and ...Ng6 maneuver to capture that weak e5-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.e3  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/a943b1dd-02ba-4622-be8d-d6786b8dee56/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 1 we saw that White can easily get in trouble if they aren't ready for our Albin Countergambit. If we manage to push ...d4 and give a check on b4 we could already be in good shape. However, when White knows what they're doing, they will prevent ...Bb4+ with a3 on move 5. And that is what Chapter 2 is all about. Let's begin, with 1.d4 for White and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts. And we'll push the pawn to d4. We already know that we want to give a check on b4 if White goes 4.Nf3 and 5.e3.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White plays Nf3 first. We'll defend the pawn. Now ...Bb4+ is an idea.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {Which is why the most annoying line from White is 5.a3, played just over 20% of the time, in over 330,000 games. White stops Bb4+, forcing us to change our plans. So we'll stop any potential White b4 advance by playing a5. Then we'll bring our bishop to c5, supporting d4. Apart from that, our ...Nge7 and ...Ng6 idea to target the e5-pawn is still viable. Other ideas to mention here for Black are 5...f6 to develop rapidly, 5...Bg4, planning ...Qe7 and ...O-O-O, and 5...Nge7 to go for the e5-pawn.} 5...a5 6.e3 {In this study, White challenges the center with e3 and opens up the bishop. This move is much better now that they have a pawn on a3. It's played 40% of the time. As mentioned, we'll bring the bishop to support the center.} 6...Bc5 7.exd4 {Expect to see this trade as White wants to get rid of our problematic d4-pawn. We'll recapture with the bishop, attacking the e5-pawn.} 7...Bxd4 8.Nxd4 {White chooses to capture again. We'll take with the queen, maintaining the attack on the e5-pawn. White could easily go wrong here.} 8...Qxd4 9.Qxd4 {Accepting the queen trade is already an inaccuracy. We'll recapture, and our knight arrives at the d4-square where it is threatening to jump to the problematic light squares on c2 and b3.} 9...Nxd4 10.Ra2 {Basically the only good move for White is Ra2, evading the forks. Other moves like 10.Bd3 or 10.Kd1 lose on the spot! We'll develop the bishop, and we are attacking the knight since the rook has moved.} 10...Bf5 11.Nc3 {Of course, White will move it to c3. But we have a concrete plan.} 11...O-O-O {And that's to dominate the e-file by placing the rook there after the knight maneuver. We will probably capture the e5-pawn and even if we don't, we can challenge it with ...f6 since White's king is on the e-file. Our knight is still eyeing those c2- and b3-squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.e3  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/a943b1dd-02ba-4622-be8d-d6786b8dee56/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 1 we saw that White can easily get in trouble if they aren't ready for our Albin Countergambit. If we manage to push ...d4 and give a check on b4 we could already be in good shape. However, when White knows what they're doing, they will prevent ...Bb4+ with a3 on move 5. And that is what Chapter 2 is all about. Let's begin, with 1.d4 for White and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts. And we'll push the pawn to d4. We already know that we want to give a check on b4 if White goes 4.Nf3 and 5.e3.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White plays Nf3 first. We'll defend the pawn. Now ...Bb4+ is an idea.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {Which is why the most annoying line from White is 5.a3, played just over 20% of the time, in over 330,000 games. White stops Bb4+, forcing us to change our plans. So we'll stop any potential White b4 advance by playing a5. Then we'll bring our bishop to c5, supporting d4. Apart from that, our ...Nge7 and ...Ng6 idea to target the e5-pawn is still viable. Other ideas to mention here for Black are 5...f6 to develop rapidly, 5...Bg4, planning ...Qe7 and ...O-O-O, and 5...Nge7 to go for the e5-pawn.} 5...a5 6.e3 {In this study, White challenges the center with e3 and opens up the bishop. This move is much better now that they have a pawn on a3. It's played 40% of the time. As mentioned, we'll bring the bishop to support the center.} 6...Bc5 7.exd4 {Expect to see this trade as White wants to get rid of our problematic d4-pawn. We'll recapture with the bishop, attacking the e5-pawn.} 7...Bxd4 8.Be2 {We'll analyze Be2 here again as an alternative to trading in the center. White may want to keep pieces on the board thinking they can get an advantage and castle. We don't have to rush with capturing the e5-pawn and risking losing castling rights. Let's bring the knight to e7 instead and castle on the next move.} 8...Nge7 9.O-O {White castles... And so will we. Now we intend capturing on e5 in the near future.} 9...O-O 10.Nxd4 {White continuing to trade here will only give us more targets. Let's offer to trade queens as well.} 10...Qxd4 11.Qxd4 {White accepts. We are happy to exchange queens because now the knight on d4 is a monster. It's attacking the bishop on e2 and threatening to jump to c2 or b3.} 11...Nxd4 12.Bd1 {Which is why White retreats and covers both squares. But the bishop no longer protects the c4-pawn. So we will attack it with Be6!} 12...Be6 13.b3 {White defends the c4-pawn...} 13...a4 {But not for long. We'll challenge that structure right away and we've easily equalized.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.e3  / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/a943b1dd-02ba-4622-be8d-d6786b8dee56/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 1 we saw that White can easily get in trouble if they aren't ready for our Albin Countergambit. If we manage to push ...d4 and give a check on b4 we could already be in good shape. However, when White knows what they're doing, they will prevent ...Bb4+ with a3 on move 5. And that is what Chapter 2 is all about. Let's begin, with 1.d4 for White and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts. And we'll push the pawn to d4. We already know that we want to give a check on b4 if White goes 4.Nf3 and 5.e3.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White plays Nf3 first. We'll defend the pawn. Now ...Bb4+ is an idea.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {Which is why the most annoying line from White is 5.a3, played just over 20% of the time, in over 330,000 games. White stops Bb4+, forcing us to change our plans. So we'll stop any potential White b4 advance by playing a5. Then we'll bring our bishop to c5, supporting d4. Apart from that, our ...Nge7 and ...Ng6 idea to target the e5-pawn is still viable. Other ideas to mention here for Black are 5...f6 to develop rapidly, 5...Bg4, planning ...Qe7 and ...O-O-O, and 5...Nge7 to go for the e5-pawn.} 5...a5 6.e3 {In this study, White challenges the center with e3 and opens up the bishop. This move is much better now that they have a pawn on a3. It's played 40% of the time. As mentioned, we'll bring the bishop to support the center.} 6...Bc5 7.exd4 {Expect to see this trade as White wants to get rid of our problematic d4-pawn. We'll recapture with the bishop, attacking the e5-pawn.} 7...Bxd4 8.Be2 {We'll analyze Be2 here again as an alternative to trading in the center. White may want to keep pieces on the board thinking they can get an advantage and castle. We don't have to rush with capturing the e5-pawn and risking losing castling rights. Let's bring the knight to e7 instead and castle on the next move.} 8...Nge7 9.O-O {White castles... And so will we. Now we intend capturing on e5 in the near future.} 9...O-O 10.Nc3 {Again, White could abstain from trading. Which is fine for us. We'll take the knight and damage their pawn structure further.} 10...Bxc3 11.bxc3 {The pawn recaptures...} 11...Ng6 {And now White can't save the e5-pawn. The c-pawns are also weak and clear targets.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.e3  / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/a943b1dd-02ba-4622-be8d-d6786b8dee56/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 1 we saw that White can easily get in trouble if they aren't ready for our Albin Countergambit. If we manage to push ...d4 and give a check on b4 we could already be in good shape. However, when White knows what they're doing, they will prevent ...Bb4+ with a3 on move 5. And that is what Chapter 2 is all about. Let's begin, with 1.d4 for White and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts. And we'll push the pawn to d4. We already know that we want to give a check on b4 if White goes 4.Nf3 and 5.e3.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White plays Nf3 first. We'll defend the pawn. Now ...Bb4+ is an idea.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {Which is why the most annoying line from White is 5.a3, played just over 20% of the time, in over 330,000 games. White stops Bb4+, forcing us to change our plans. So we'll stop any potential White b4 advance by playing a5. Then we'll bring our bishop to c5, supporting d4. Apart from that, our ...Nge7 and ...Ng6 idea to target the e5-pawn is still viable. Other ideas to mention here for Black are 5...f6 to develop rapidly, 5...Bg4, planning ...Qe7 and ...O-O-O, and 5...Nge7 to go for the e5-pawn.} 5...a5 6.e3 {In this study, White challenges the center with e3 and opens up the bishop. This move is much better now that they have a pawn on a3. It's played 40% of the time. As mentioned, we'll bring the bishop to support the center.} 6...Bc5 7.Be2 {White could also go the passive route and activate the bishop to e2. In that case, we'll force them to capture in the center. We'll play ...Bf5, threatening to push the pawn to d3.} 7...Bf5 8.exd4 {White basically has to take now. The trades in the center will help us because of the vulnerable e5-pawn. We'll recapture with the knight.} 8...Nxd4 9.Nxd4 {White takes on d4 again...} 9...Qxd4 {And I suggest we take with the queen here. This is flexible because it attacks the e5-pawn and prepares queenside castling at the same time. Black is doing fine here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Nbd2 and 6.g3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/6ae2c91a-4d48-475f-825a-614d98cfd2d0/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study will show the two most popular alternatives to e3 on move 6 for White. Instead of challenging our d4-pawn, White can opt for different developing ideas, such as activating the knight to d2 or going for the light-squared bishop fianchetto. Let's begin, with 1.d4 by White and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White offers the Queen's Gambit. We'll reply with the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit. And we'll push ...d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White attacks the pawn. We'll bring out our knight. It's the best way to protect it.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {White goes for the annoying 5.a3. Let's prevent b4 and expand on the queenside, awaiting White's next move.} 5...a5 6.Nbd2 {White players choose 6.Nbd2 15% of the time. The idea is to activate the knight quickly, and aim for Nb3 to pressure our d4-pawn, claiming it's overextended. A flexible response is ...Nge7. We can both rotate the knight to g6 and attack the e5-pawn or protect the d4-pawn from f5 if White attacks it.} 6...Nge7 7.Nb3 {White goes for the attack on the pawn. And we'll protect it with Nf5. White has certainly forced us to switch our setup a little bit, but we have a clear idea of pushing the a-pawn and dislodging the knight from b3.} 7...Nf5 8.Bg5 {White can continue attacking our pieces and grab some momentum here. We won't be able to activate the dark-squared bishop as we'd like. But a trade is fine. We will get a lot of counterplay.} 8...Be7 9.Bxe7 {White captures. We'll take back with the queen.} 9...Qxe7 10.Nbxd4 {And now the knight finally captures on d4. White has captured the strong central pawn, but at what price? We'll trade once.} 10...Nfxd4 11.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. Now we'll castle. White is currently up two pawns, but we have a big lead in development and are threatening to pin the knight with ...Rd8. Also, the e5-pawn will soon fall.} 11...O-O 12.Nxc6 {So, White trades on c6. But they traded off their only active piece.} 12...bxc6 {We'll recapture, with easy ideas like ...Rd8 to attack the queen and ...Rb8 to pressure the queenside since our a-pawn is already restricting White from pushing. The e5-pawn is likely to fall shortly. White is yet to activate their pieces and we are doing fine.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Nbd2 and 6.g3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/6ae2c91a-4d48-475f-825a-614d98cfd2d0/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study will show the two most popular alternatives to e3 on move 6 for White. Instead of challenging our d4-pawn, White can opt for different developing ideas, such as activating the knight to d2 or going for the light-squared bishop fianchetto. Let's begin, with 1.d4 by White and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White offers the Queen's Gambit. We'll reply with the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit. And we'll push ...d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White attacks the pawn. We'll bring out our knight. It's the best way to protect it.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {White goes for the annoying 5.a3. Let's prevent b4 and expand on the queenside, awaiting White's next move.} 5...a5 6.Nbd2 {White players choose 6.Nbd2 15% of the time. The idea is to activate the knight quickly, and aim for Nb3 to pressure our d4-pawn, claiming it's overextended. A flexible response is ...Nge7. We can both rotate the knight to g6 and attack the e5-pawn or protect the d4-pawn from f5 if White attacks it.} 6...Nge7 7.Nb3 {White goes for the attack on the pawn. And we'll protect it with Nf5. White has certainly forced us to switch our setup a little bit, but we have a clear idea of pushing the a-pawn and dislodging the knight from b3.} 7...Nf5 8.g4 {8.g4 is not the best move for White. It looks like White is forcing our knight to move away and that we will simply lose the e5-pawn. But it's not that easy. Let's rotate the knight to h4, offering a trade.} 8...Nh4 9.Nbxd4 {White will most likely capture on d4, which was their idea after all. But they have left themselves vulnerable to a couple of trades, and the g4-pawn is now hanging. We'll capture on f3 with check.} 9...Nxf3+ 10.Nxf3 {White has to recapture with the knight. And now we'll trade queens.} 10...Qxd1+ 11.Kxd1 {The king recaptures...} 11...Bxg4 {And we get a pawn back, already attacking the defender of the e5-pawn. We can still castle, unlike White, and our bishop can come to c5 to attack the f2-pawn. White has gained nothing playing like this.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Nbd2 and 6.g3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/6ae2c91a-4d48-475f-825a-614d98cfd2d0/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study will show the two most popular alternatives to e3 on move 6 for White. Instead of challenging our d4-pawn, White can opt for different developing ideas, such as activating the knight to d2 or going for the light-squared bishop fianchetto. Let's begin, with 1.d4 by White and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White offers the Queen's Gambit. We'll reply with the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit. And we'll push ...d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White attacks the pawn. We'll bring out our knight. It's the best way to protect it.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {White goes for the annoying 5.a3. Let's prevent b4 and expand on the queenside, awaiting White's next move.} 5...a5 6.Nbd2 {White players choose 6.Nbd2 15% of the time. The idea is to activate the knight quickly, and aim for Nb3 to pressure our d4-pawn, claiming it's overextended. A flexible response is ...Nge7. We can both rotate the knight to g6 and attack the e5-pawn or protect the d4-pawn from f5 if White attacks it.} 6...Nge7 7.Nb3 {White goes for the attack on the pawn. And we'll protect it with Nf5. White has certainly forced us to switch our setup a little bit, but we have a clear idea of pushing the a-pawn and dislodging the knight from b3.} 7...Nf5 8.e4 {An active move White can choose is e4, attacking our knight. We are forced to capture en passant, otherwise we would lose the d4-pawn.} 8...dxe3 9.Qxd8+ {White trades queens first. The benefit for them is that we recapture with the king and lose the castling rights. Our knight is too valuable on c6 to move it away.} 9...Kxd8 10.fxe3 {And now the pawn recaptures. White doesn't want to take with the bishop and let us have the bishop pair. But we will play a4 and attack the knight on b3.} 10...a4 11.Nbd4 {White can jump to the center in this line, having retaken control of d4. We don't want to capture and fix their pawn structure. Instead, developing the bishop and going for activity is better, even at the cost of ruining our pawn structure.} 11...Bc5 12.Nxc6+ {White trades knights on c6. We'll recapture with the pawn. Our c-pawns are vulnerable, but White has left themselves with a lot of targets as well, and our rook may be well-placed on b8 in the near future. Meanwhile, the e3-pawn is currently under attack.} 12...bxc6 13.Kf2 {So White protects it with the king.} 13...Re8 {And we'll slide the rook to e8, now targeting the e5-pawn. The key in this line was going for activity. We will continue to target White's weaknesses with moves like ...Rb8, attacking the backwards b2-pawn, and ...g5 with the idea of ...g4 to remove the defender of e5. Our bishop can join the game on d7 and our king can hide on c8.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Nbd2 and 6.g3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/6ae2c91a-4d48-475f-825a-614d98cfd2d0/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study will show the two most popular alternatives to e3 on move 6 for White. Instead of challenging our d4-pawn, White can opt for different developing ideas, such as activating the knight to d2 or going for the light-squared bishop fianchetto. Let's begin, with 1.d4 by White and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White offers the Queen's Gambit. We'll reply with the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit. And we'll push ...d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White attacks the pawn. We'll bring out our knight. It's the best way to protect it.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {White goes for the annoying 5.a3. Let's prevent b4 and expand on the queenside, awaiting White's next move.} 5...a5 6.g3 {If White prefers to activate the light-squared bishop before the queenside knight, they will play g3 to prepare Bg2. This happens just under 15% of the time. We'll continue with our normal plan of developing and supporting the center. The knight is coming to e7.} 6...Bc5 7.Bg2 {White activates the bishop and prepares castling. And we prepare to site our king's knight on g6, where it will take aim at the e5-pawn.} 7...Nge7 8.O-O {White castles. We will do likewise. Our rook can be useful later on e8.} 8...O-O 9.Nbd2 {White finally brings their knight to d2. Now the threat of Nb3 is not nearly as powerful, especially when our knight is already on e7. We'll can jump to g6 and attack the e5-pawn before White has time to act.} 9...Ng6 10.Ne4 {In this case, White centralizes the knight and attacks our bishop. We are not troubled. White's move does nothing to prevent us from ultimately capturing on e5. Our d4-pawn is still sufficiently defended.} 10...Be7 11.Bf4 {Finally, White protects the pawn. But which of their pieces does a good job both defending and attacking? The knight on f3.} 11...Bg4 {Which is why we will pressure it with ...Bg4. There's a lot of chess left to play, but this is our optimal setup when White plays g3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 3: White Plays 6.Bf4 and 6.b3  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/6a42cc87-38e2-4589-a350-47452789fe7a/lines"]

1.d4 {The final study of this chapter will show two more options for White on move 6, which are 6.Bf4 and 6.b3. They have to do with the dark-squared bishop being developed to either f4 or b2 and White focusing on either the defense of their e5-pawn or the attack on our d4-pawn. We'll start off with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Now both pawns are in the center. We'll play the Albin Countergambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White captures on e5. Let's push our d-pawn.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White attacks the pawn. We'll develop and defend it.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {And White prevents ...Bb4. We'll expand on the queenside.} 5...a5 6.b3 {6.b3 is played just under 10% of the time, and the plan is simple. White wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and harass our pawn on d4. We'll anticipate that and protect it with our own bishop.} 6...Bc5 7.Bb2 {White goes for the fianchetto anyway. The bishop has left the c1-h6 diagonal, so I suggest we act quickly now. Our pawn is defended for now and we can use White's setup to develop rapidly. Let's challenge the center with the f-pawn.} 7...f6 8.exf6 {White has little choice and has to capture. We'll recapture with the knight, of course. This variation will allow us to place our pieces on their optimal squares without any concern.} 8...Nxf6 9.g3 {White goes for g3 with the idea of Bg2. Another slow developing process. We'll activate our light-squared bishop in the meantime.} 9...Bg4 10.Bg2 {White defends. What's the best square for our queen now? It's the e7-square, taking more control of the e-file. White can't capture on d4 because of checkmate on e2 after all the trades.} 10...Qe7 11.O-O {So they castle. Now the d4-pawn is actually under attack.} 11...Rd8 {So we'll protect it with the rook. Castling is next and we already have an enjoyable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 3: White Plays 6.Bf4 and 6.b3  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/6a42cc87-38e2-4589-a350-47452789fe7a/lines"]

1.d4 {The final study of this chapter will show two more options for White on move 6, which are 6.Bf4 and 6.b3. They have to do with the dark-squared bishop being developed to either f4 or b2 and White focusing on either the defense of their e5-pawn or the attack on our d4-pawn. We'll start off with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Now both pawns are in the center. We'll play the Albin Countergambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White captures on e5. Let's push our d-pawn.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White attacks the pawn. We'll develop and defend it.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {And White prevents ...Bb4. We'll expand on the queenside.} 5...a5 6.Bf4 {White might anticipate our plans to attack the e5-pawn and preemptively develop the bishop to f4 to defend. This happens 12% of the time. And this idea by White makes our ...Nge7/...Ng6 maneuver even more attractive because the knight will also attack the bishop on g6.} 6...Nge7 7.Nbd2 {White activates the knight to d2. It might come to e4 in some cases. We will get our knight to g6, of course.} 7...Ng6 8.Bg3 {White will have to waste another tempo moving away the bishop. And that gives us further ideas to expand and keep the momentum. We will advance the h-pawn and threaten ...h4!} 8...h5 9.h3 {The worse, though more common, option is h3. White simply wants a square for the bishop without any confrontation. Why not grab space when we're allowed? We'll push h4.} 9...h4 10.Bh2 {The bishop retreats. We've grabbed a lot of space on the kingside. How about the queenside?} 10...a4 {We can do the same with ...a4! White's position is completely restricted now, while we have easy means for development. Our bishops will activate to f5 and c5 and we will focus on the e5-pawn in the beginning of the middlegame. We will do so with ...Rh5 and ...Qe7. White probably won't be able to save it.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Annoying 5.a3 / Study 3: White Plays 6.Bf4 and 6.b3  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/838e6516-c9e9-413b-9ea4-89387e25d825/studies/6a42cc87-38e2-4589-a350-47452789fe7a/lines"]

1.d4 {The final study of this chapter will show two more options for White on move 6, which are 6.Bf4 and 6.b3. They have to do with the dark-squared bishop being developed to either f4 or b2 and White focusing on either the defense of their e5-pawn or the attack on our d4-pawn. We'll start off with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Now both pawns are in the center. We'll play the Albin Countergambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White captures on e5. Let's push our d-pawn.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White attacks the pawn. We'll develop and defend it.} 4...Nc6 5.a3 {And White prevents ...Bb4. We'll expand on the queenside.} 5...a5 6.Bf4 {White might anticipate our plans to attack the e5-pawn and preemptively develop the bishop to f4 to defend. This happens 12% of the time. And this idea by White makes our ...Nge7/...Ng6 maneuver even more attractive because the knight will also attack the bishop on g6.} 6...Nge7 7.Nbd2 {White activates the knight to d2. It might come to e4 in some cases. We will get our knight to g6, of course.} 7...Ng6 8.Bg3 {White will have to waste another tempo moving away the bishop. And that gives us further ideas to expand and keep the momentum. We will advance the h-pawn and threaten ...h4!} 8...h5 9.h4 {A more active approach from White is blocking our h-pawn with h4, although White could get themselves in trouble if not careful. We'll continue with the standard idea of grabbing space on the queenside and await White's next move.} 9...a4 10.Ne4 {They decide to centralize the knight. They might be eyeing the g5-square. Let's attack the knight and actually provoke it to jump to g5.} 10...Bf5 11.Neg5 {Because that move is a mistake! White might think their knight is incredibly active and that we can't kick it out... But we will play ...f6. The position is not open enough that this weakens our king.} 11...f6 12.exf6 {White captures once. We'll recapture, attacking the knight again. Our point is that the knight doesn't have many squares to retreat to.} 12...gxf6 13.Nh3 {In fact, h3 is the only square they have. Our sequence of moves give us a lot of activity.} 13...Bh6 {Now Bh6 restricts the knight on h3. Look at our bishops eyeing the queenside and controlling some key squares in White's camp. We also have the space advantage and our d4-pawn is protected. Meanwhile, White's pieces are stuck together and passive. Black is certainly better, because White will have to play e3 or e4, allowing ...dxe4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Fianchetto Variation (5.g3) / Study 1: White Plays 6.b3  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/f04ecc74-cb6a-42b1-94a0-65fbd95a563b/studies/29042bdb-036e-4462-af30-dc0a3a39c92b/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 3 will focus on White's early fianchetto with 5.g3, as opposed to 5.a3. It is certainly a viable plan where White aims to develop the kingside quickly without confrontation on the kingside or in the center. However, the first study which deals with 6.b3, or the double fianchetto, will demonstrate how easily we can attack the king in the Albin! Let's begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll reply with the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White takes the gambit pawn. We'll push forward with d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {The standard attack on the central pawn. And we'll deploy the standard defense.} 4...Nc6 5.g3 {And now White goes for the fianchetto idea, which is played just under 20% of the time, in more than 275,000 games. White hasn't played e3, so ...Bb4+ doesn't work here. But notice that once White's bishop leaves the f1-square, the c4-pawn isn't defended by it, so we will counter White's plans by attacking that pawn! Other ideas involve playing on the kingside together with the queen.} 5...Be6 6.b3 {White creates the chain and wants to go for a double fianchetto, which is actually a mistake! It's the most common move and it's played around 35% of the time. The main problem for White in this setup is they have had to make four moves in total just to fianchetto both bishops. In the meantime, we will develop rapidly, in contrast to White's leisurely approach. Here, we are already creating a queen/bishop battery and our bishop is coming to h3 to trade soon.} 6...Qd7 7.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto anyway. There is no going back now. Having activated the queen, we can castle long. This reinforces the d-file and will help us push the pawns on the kingside and eventually attack.} 7...O-O-O 8.O-O {White castles. Everything is going according to plan. We'll propose the bishop trade here. Once we do trade, our ideas will revolve around pushing ...h5 and supporting it with a knight on g6, from where it will also be attacking the center.} 8...Bh3 9.Bb2 {White finally develops both bishops, but they are already slightly worse here. We'll put our plan into motion with ...h5.} 9...h5 10.Nbd2 {White finally develops the queenside knight, but this is all too slow. Going for Bb2 was unnecessary as our d4-pawn is defended and White can't afford to waste a move on that when they have to defend. We'll exchange the bishops and get ready to open up the white king.} 10...Bxg2 11.Kxg2 {The king recaptures. And ...h4 is coming. We want to open up the h-file.} 11...h4 12.Nxh4 {So White captures with the knight. Don't rush here and sacrifice the rook. It looks tempting but we need that rook. Instead, attacking the knight with g5 will suffice.} 12...g5 13.Nhf3 {White has to retreat to f3. And our queen can infiltrate with check.} 13...Qh3+ 14.Kg1 {White moves back to g1. We need to find a way to remove the knight from f3 so that we can checkmate on h2. Pushing the g-pawn does attack the knight, but...} 14...g4 15.Nh4 {White can jump back to the h-file, blocking the rook's vision. We counted on that, though.} 15...Be7 {We have ...Be7, attacking the knight! White is busted and we will win soon down the open h-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Fianchetto Variation (5.g3) / Study 1: White Plays 6.b3  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/f04ecc74-cb6a-42b1-94a0-65fbd95a563b/studies/29042bdb-036e-4462-af30-dc0a3a39c92b/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 3 will focus on White's early fianchetto with 5.g3, as opposed to 5.a3. It is certainly a viable plan where White aims to develop the kingside quickly without confrontation on the kingside or in the center. However, the first study which deals with 6.b3, or the double fianchetto, will demonstrate how easily we can attack the king in the Albin! Let's begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll reply with the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White takes the gambit pawn. We'll push forward with d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {The standard attack on the central pawn. And we'll deploy the standard defense.} 4...Nc6 5.g3 {And now White goes for the fianchetto idea, which is played just under 20% of the time, in more than 275,000 games. White hasn't played e3, so ...Bb4+ doesn't work here. But notice that once White's bishop leaves the f1-square, the c4-pawn isn't defended by it, so we will counter White's plans by attacking that pawn! Other ideas involve playing on the kingside together with the queen.} 5...Be6 6.b3 {White creates the chain and wants to go for a double fianchetto, which is actually a mistake! It's the most common move and it's played around 35% of the time. The main problem for White in this setup is they have had to make four moves in total just to fianchetto both bishops. In the meantime, we will develop rapidly, in contrast to White's leisurely approach. Here, we are already creating a queen/bishop battery and our bishop is coming to h3 to trade soon.} 6...Qd7 7.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto anyway. There is no going back now. Having activated the queen, we can castle long. This reinforces the d-file and will help us push the pawns on the kingside and eventually attack.} 7...O-O-O 8.O-O {White castles. Everything is going according to plan. We'll propose the bishop trade here. Once we do trade, our ideas will revolve around pushing ...h5 and supporting it with a knight on g6, from where it will also be attacking the center.} 8...Bh3 9.Bb2 {White finally develops both bishops, but they are already slightly worse here. We'll put our plan into motion with ...h5.} 9...h5 10.Nbd2 {White finally develops the queenside knight, but this is all too slow. Going for Bb2 was unnecessary as our d4-pawn is defended and White can't afford to waste a move on that when they have to defend. We'll exchange the bishops and get ready to open up the white king.} 10...Bxg2 11.Kxg2 {The king recaptures. And ...h4 is coming. We want to open up the h-file.} 11...h4 12.Rh1 {White can also occupy the h-file with their rook and try to defend. In that case, we can obtain a winning advantage from a positional perspective. Let's push ...h3 and check the king. It does block the h-file, but our main idea isn't to attack anymore.} 12...h3+ 13.Kf1 {White slides to f1 and now the rook is in jail, which is why we will eventually be winning. White won't be getting it out soon. Meanwhile, we can start opening up files in the center. Let's play ...f6 and provoke the trade.} 13...f6 14.exf6 {White captures...} 14...Nxf6 {And our knight gets to f6. The White king is still weak and we will get the rooks to the e- and the f-file against it. Our bishop can develop to c5 and reinforce the center. Black is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Fianchetto Variation (5.g3) / Study 1: White Plays 6.b3  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/f04ecc74-cb6a-42b1-94a0-65fbd95a563b/studies/29042bdb-036e-4462-af30-dc0a3a39c92b/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 3 will focus on White's early fianchetto with 5.g3, as opposed to 5.a3. It is certainly a viable plan where White aims to develop the kingside quickly without confrontation on the kingside or in the center. However, the first study which deals with 6.b3, or the double fianchetto, will demonstrate how easily we can attack the king in the Albin! Let's begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll reply with the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White takes the gambit pawn. We'll push forward with d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {The standard attack on the central pawn. And we'll deploy the standard defense.} 4...Nc6 5.g3 {And now White goes for the fianchetto idea, which is played just under 20% of the time, in more than 275,000 games. White hasn't played e3, so ...Bb4+ doesn't work here. But notice that once White's bishop leaves the f1-square, the c4-pawn isn't defended by it, so we will counter White's plans by attacking that pawn! Other ideas involve playing on the kingside together with the queen.} 5...Be6 6.b3 {White creates the chain and wants to go for a double fianchetto, which is actually a mistake! It's the most common move and it's played around 35% of the time. The main problem for White in this setup is they have had to make four moves in total just to fianchetto both bishops. In the meantime, we will develop rapidly, in contrast to White's leisurely approach. Here, we are already creating a queen/bishop battery and our bishop is coming to h3 to trade soon.} 6...Qd7 7.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto anyway. There is no going back now. Having activated the queen, we can castle long. This reinforces the d-file and will help us push the pawns on the kingside and eventually attack.} 7...O-O-O 8.O-O {White castles. Everything is going according to plan. We'll propose the bishop trade here. Once we do trade, our ideas will revolve around pushing ...h5 and supporting it with a knight on g6, from where it will also be attacking the center.} 8...Bh3 9.Nbd2 {A somewhat better approach for White is developing the knight to d2 here. But even that could get real dangerous really quickly if White isn't accurate. We'll continue with our plan and push ...h5.} 9...h5 10.Ne4 {White centralizes the knight to help out. We'll exchange bishops on g2.} 10...Bxg2 11.Kxg2 {The king recaptures, and you can probably feel our next move. It's ...h4! We want to open up the h-file and infiltrate with ...Qh3+. The best move for White here is Neg5 to take control of the h3-square, but that move is hard to play.} 11...h4 12.Nxh4 {Instead, if White captures on h4, they are already on the verge of being lost. The knight is the only piece preventing us from getting the h-file. So let's attack it. Again, White can try to hold the game with Ng5.} 12...Be7 13.Nf3 {But retreating to f3 loses on the spot. Our queen finally arrives at h3 with check.} 13...Qh3+ 14.Kg1 {White might think we can't checkmate because the knight is covering the h2-square.} 14...Nxe5 {But we can capture on e5 and threaten to capture that knight! White can't capture because of checkmate, and if they retreat with Ned2, we can even play ...Ng4. Black is completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Fianchetto Variation (5.g3) / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bg2 and 6.Nbd2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/f04ecc74-cb6a-42b1-94a0-65fbd95a563b/studies/32368363-13e4-46c8-ae00-df3b444e8737/lines"]

1.d4 {In Study 2, we will look at some better responses for White on move 6, such as 6.Bg2 and 6.Nbd2. 6.Nbd2 is quicker in terms of central presence and defense, while 6.Bg2 gives up the c4-pawn completely but is actually the best move! Let's take a look, beginning with 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The standard second move. We'll go for the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White captures. We'll push the pawn.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White attacks it. And we'll develop, protecting it.} 4...Nc6 5.g3 {White is going for the quick fianchetto in this chapter. We will pressure the abandoned c4-pawn.} 5...Be6 6.Nbd2 {6.Nbd2 is played just over a quarter of the time. White defends the pawn by developing and avoids the slow and unnecessary b3 and Bb2. Our plans will stay similar, though. We will create the battery anyway. We want to trade the light-squared bishops and castle long.} 6...Qd7 7.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. And we'll castle to bring the king to safety and activate the rook.} 7...O-O-O 8.O-O {White castles as well. This is a sound defensive system for White. We will push the h-pawn, starting an immediate attack. Another idea here was the standard ...Nge7 and ...Ng6 to go after the e5-pawn.} 8...h5 9.h4 {However, White can now play h4 as there is no bishop on h3. We have to resort to other means of attack. How to open the necessary files? We can challenge the center with ...f6!} 9...f6 10.exf6 {White captures. And we will recapture with the pawn because we want to use the g-file! This is important.} 10...gxf6 11.a3 {On the other hand, White's usual ideas involve queenside expansion. To each their own. We can finally offer the bishop trade now.} 11...Bh3 12.b4 {White goes for b4 anyway.} 12...Nh6 {And we'll continue kingside operations. The rook is coming to the g-file and the position is unclear, with a lot more chess to be played.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Fianchetto Variation (5.g3) / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bg2 and 6.Nbd2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/f04ecc74-cb6a-42b1-94a0-65fbd95a563b/studies/32368363-13e4-46c8-ae00-df3b444e8737/lines"]

1.d4 {In Study 2, we will look at some better responses for White on move 6, such as 6.Bg2 and 6.Nbd2. 6.Nbd2 is quicker in terms of central presence and defense, while 6.Bg2 gives up the c4-pawn completely but is actually the best move! Let's take a look, beginning with 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The standard second move. We'll go for the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White captures. We'll push the pawn.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {White attacks it. And we'll develop, protecting it.} 4...Nc6 5.g3 {White is going for the quick fianchetto in this chapter. We will pressure the abandoned c4-pawn.} 5...Be6 6.Bg2 {According to the computer, the best move is to actually go for rapid development and ignore the c4-pawn. It's played around 30% of the time. In fact, we shouldn't even waste time with the pawn. Let's stick to our plan. And that's the well-known queen/bishop battery.} 6...Qd7 7.O-O {White castles...} 7...Nge7 {And we'll bring the knight to e7. This is a good line to demonstrate the standard moves we want to play in the 5.g3 variations of the Albin. The knight is coming to g6 and we will be pushing the h-pawn soon. The bishop is also coming to h3 to trade.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Alternatives on Move 5 / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bf4 and 5.Nbd2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/27ce5d3d-15c8-4356-813f-1e609ca9b9fe/studies/049dc08a-716b-44e6-820d-ceea36d6d0df/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 4 of the Albin Countergambit, we will look at some fifth move deviations by White. Instead of 5.a3 or the 4.Nf3/ 5.e3 setups, White can choose some other options to try to counter our Albin aggression. You will see a lot of familiar motifs in this chapter. Study 1 will deal with 5.Bf4 and 5.Nbd2. Let's begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's go for the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White captures... And we'll push, of course.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {The knight comes out. And we'll protect the pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nbd2 {A move that's played equally as often as 5.Bf4 is White developing the knight to d2. The knight might want to come to b3 quickly and attack our pawn. So I suggest we act immediately and challenge the center in gambit spirit! This will accelerate our development and provide enough resources for a battle in the center. An alternative was ...Nge7.} 5...f6 6.exf6 {White captures, naturally. We'll take back with the knight.} 6...Nxf6 7.Nb3 {White goes for the attack on the pawn. When this happens, your main ideas should revolve around giving a check on the b4-square. This will force White to block with the bishop which will actually give life to our position in the long run.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Bd2 {White blocks with the bishop. We'll trade.} 8...Bxd2+ 9.Qxd2 {The queen comes to d2, and our pawn is still under attack. Let's centralize the knight and attack the queen, though.} 9...Ne4 10.Qf4 {White doesn't have time to capture, so slides the queen to f4... In this position, we are giving away the d4-pawn. But our play is on the b4-square, where we want to place our queen, therefore we'll play Qe7, also defending our knight. White has to be careful because the most natural moves lose the game on the spot.} 10...Qe7 11.Nbxd4 {White takes on d4. This loses. Let's trade knights.} 11...Nxd4 12.Nxd4 {Our idea is that when the knight leaves the f3-square... We can attack the queen from f8, while still threatening the check on b4!} 12...Rf8 13.Qe3 {The queen doesn't have many squares to go to. White certainly can't stop us from playing Qb4+. It is an important move because White has to move the king and keep it weak and in the center.} 13...Qb4+ 14.Kd1 {White slides to d1.} 14...Bf5 {And we are easily winning after ...Bf5. The bishop protects the knight and we will castle on the next move, bringing the rook to the dangerous d-file. White will not be able to defend.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Alternatives on Move 5 / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bf4 and 5.Nbd2 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/27ce5d3d-15c8-4356-813f-1e609ca9b9fe/studies/049dc08a-716b-44e6-820d-ceea36d6d0df/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 4 of the Albin Countergambit, we will look at some fifth move deviations by White. Instead of 5.a3 or the 4.Nf3/ 5.e3 setups, White can choose some other options to try to counter our Albin aggression. You will see a lot of familiar motifs in this chapter. Study 1 will deal with 5.Bf4 and 5.Nbd2. Let's begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's go for the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White captures... And we'll push, of course.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {The knight comes out. And we'll protect the pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nbd2 {A move that's played equally as often as 5.Bf4 is White developing the knight to d2. The knight might want to come to b3 quickly and attack our pawn. So I suggest we act immediately and challenge the center in gambit spirit! This will accelerate our development and provide enough resources for a battle in the center. An alternative was ...Nge7.} 5...f6 6.exf6 {White captures, naturally. We'll take back with the knight.} 6...Nxf6 7.Nb3 {White goes for the attack on the pawn. When this happens, your main ideas should revolve around giving a check on the b4-square. This will force White to block with the bishop which will actually give life to our position in the long run.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Bd2 {White blocks with the bishop. We'll trade.} 8...Bxd2+ 9.Qxd2 {The queen comes to d2, and our pawn is still under attack. Let's centralize the knight and attack the queen, though.} 9...Ne4 10.Qf4 {White doesn't have time to capture, so slides the queen to f4... In this position, we are giving away the d4-pawn. But our play is on the b4-square, where we want to place our queen, therefore we'll play Qe7, also defending our knight. White has to be careful because the most natural moves lose the game on the spot.} 10...Qe7 11.a3 {White could take a move and prevent any checks on b4. This is a mistake, as it gives us too much counterplay. We will attack the queen. with g5. Amazingly enough, it only has one free square to move to!} 11...g5 12.Qc1 {Which is c1. We have completely turned the game around: White was the one pressuring our position and attacking the pawn, and now we've got the momentum. We don't even have to protect the pawn. Let's play aggressively and threaten ...g4 to capture the f2-pawn.} 12...Rf8 13.h3 {They go for h3 instead to prevent ...g4.} 13...Bd7 {But our bishop can simply develop to d7, and we will castle on the next move. White's king is extremely weak and their position is passive. In fact, Black is winning!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Alternatives on Move 5 / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bf4 and 5.Nbd2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/27ce5d3d-15c8-4356-813f-1e609ca9b9fe/studies/049dc08a-716b-44e6-820d-ceea36d6d0df/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 4 of the Albin Countergambit, we will look at some fifth move deviations by White. Instead of 5.a3 or the 4.Nf3/ 5.e3 setups, White can choose some other options to try to counter our Albin aggression. You will see a lot of familiar motifs in this chapter. Study 1 will deal with 5.Bf4 and 5.Nbd2. Let's begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's go for the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White captures... And we'll push, of course.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {The knight comes out. And we'll protect the pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Bf4 {White going for 5.Bf4 will lead to similar plans for us as in the 5.a3 and 6.Bf4 lines we looked at. This is played 10% of the time in almost 200,000 games. It definitely provokes the ...Nge7 idea from us, aiming for the g6-square.} 5...Nge7 6.Nbd2 {White can't do much to stop this, so they activate the knight. And we'll fork the bishop and the pawn.} 6...Ng6 7.Bg3 {White retreats to g3. Do you remember our ideas from the chapter on 5.a3? We'll push the h-pawn and hope to trap the bishop! White basically has two ways to deal with this.} 7...h5 8.h4 {White could block our advance with h4. We'll just continue attacking the e5-pawn.} 8...Qe7 9.Nb3 {Instead of defending, White can counter attack. We can't really protect the d4-pawn now.} 9...Bf5 {What we can do is activate the bishop and prepare long castling if White captures on d4. Also, the queen is coming to b4 with check so White does have to be careful here.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Alternatives on Move 5 / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bf4 and 5.Nbd2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/27ce5d3d-15c8-4356-813f-1e609ca9b9fe/studies/049dc08a-716b-44e6-820d-ceea36d6d0df/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 4 of the Albin Countergambit, we will look at some fifth move deviations by White. Instead of 5.a3 or the 4.Nf3/ 5.e3 setups, White can choose some other options to try to counter our Albin aggression. You will see a lot of familiar motifs in this chapter. Study 1 will deal with 5.Bf4 and 5.Nbd2. Let's begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's go for the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White captures... And we'll push, of course.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {The knight comes out. And we'll protect the pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Bf4 {White going for 5.Bf4 will lead to similar plans for us as in the 5.a3 and 6.Bf4 lines we looked at. This is played 10% of the time in almost 200,000 games. It definitely provokes the ...Nge7 idea from us, aiming for the g6-square.} 5...Nge7 6.Nbd2 {White can't do much to stop this, so they activate the knight. And we'll fork the bishop and the pawn.} 6...Ng6 7.Bg3 {White retreats to g3. Do you remember our ideas from the chapter on 5.a3? We'll push the h-pawn and hope to trap the bishop! White basically has two ways to deal with this.} 7...h5 8.h3 {The one that gives us space is h3. We will still attack the bishop with h4.} 8...h4 9.Bh2 {White retreats to h2.} 9...Qe7 {And the main target is of course the e5-pawn. We already have two knights on it and if needed will add the rook as an attacker on h5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Alternatives on Move 5 / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bf4 and 5.Nbd2 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/27ce5d3d-15c8-4356-813f-1e609ca9b9fe/studies/049dc08a-716b-44e6-820d-ceea36d6d0df/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 4 of the Albin Countergambit, we will look at some fifth move deviations by White. Instead of 5.a3 or the 4.Nf3/ 5.e3 setups, White can choose some other options to try to counter our Albin aggression. You will see a lot of familiar motifs in this chapter. Study 1 will deal with 5.Bf4 and 5.Nbd2. Let's begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's go for the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White captures... And we'll push, of course.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {The knight comes out. And we'll protect the pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nbd2 {A move that's played equally as often as 5.Bf4 is White developing the knight to d2. The knight might want to come to b3 quickly and attack our pawn. So I suggest we act immediately and challenge the center in gambit spirit! This will accelerate our development and provide enough resources for a battle in the center. An alternative was ...Nge7.} 5...f6 6.exf6 {White captures, naturally. We'll take back with the knight.} 6...Nxf6 7.g3 {White could also go for the fianchetto here, and in this case we'll go for a familiar idea. We'll bring out our light-squared bishop and set up a battery.} 7...Bf5 8.Bg2 {White develops the bishop. And we will line up our queen and bishop.} 8...Qd7 9.O-O {White castles short.} 9...O-O-O {But we'll castle long. Apart from the trade on h3, ...h5 is coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Alternatives on Move 5 / Study 2: White Plays 5.Bg5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/27ce5d3d-15c8-4356-813f-1e609ca9b9fe/studies/7c95a7e5-c5d0-4e9e-9803-6a5605cb9062/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will show us how to approach 5.Bg5 by White on move 5. Using their pawn on e5, White wants to put pressure on our queen early and provoke a trade, disrupting our development. Let's see what the antidote is. We'll begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White takes. We'll push forward with d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {Attacking the pawn. We'll develop, defending it.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg5 {And White goes for this aggressive move 9% of the time. Obviously, our queen is under attack and we can't move it to a useful square. We have to block. I recommend ...f6! We offer a trade of pawns, but we open the e- and the f-file and speed up our development. This is a sharper response than ...Nge7 and might catch White off guard.} 5...f6 6.exf6 {White captures. Let's recapture with the knight.} 6...Nxf6 7.e3 {White challenging our pawn now happens a quarter of the time. It's an inaccuracy for a reason we are already familiar with. White has done nothing about the b4-square. So we'll give a check there.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Nbd2 {Now the knight has to block. It is different, however, because if we take on e3 the bishop on g5 can recapture. Which is one of the benefits of White having played 5.Bg5. That's why we will attack the bishop.} 8...h6 9.Bxf6 {White can force our hand by capturing. However, they give up the valuable bishop. We'll capture and activate our queen.} 9...Qxf6 10.exd4 {When White captures in the center, we shouldn't rush recapturing. There could be Qa4+ ideas.} 10...Bg4 {Instead, we can pin the defender of the pawn and prepare castling. No matter what, our rooks are coming to the center and we are in great shape. White probably won't save the pawn on d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Alternatives on Move 5 / Study 2: White Plays 5.Bg5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/27ce5d3d-15c8-4356-813f-1e609ca9b9fe/studies/7c95a7e5-c5d0-4e9e-9803-6a5605cb9062/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will show us how to approach 5.Bg5 by White on move 5. Using their pawn on e5, White wants to put pressure on our queen early and provoke a trade, disrupting our development. Let's see what the antidote is. We'll begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White takes. We'll push forward with d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {Attacking the pawn. We'll develop, defending it.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg5 {And White goes for this aggressive move 9% of the time. Obviously, our queen is under attack and we can't move it to a useful square. We have to block. I recommend ...f6! We offer a trade of pawns, but we open the e- and the f-file and speed up our development. This is a sharper response than ...Nge7 and might catch White off guard.} 5...f6 6.exf6 {White captures. Let's recapture with the knight.} 6...Nxf6 7.g3 {Expect to see the fianchetto here 10% of the time. White seems content with the trade on f6 and wants to develop calmly. Now the bishop doesn't have the retreating h4-square when we attack it.} 7...h6 8.Bxf6 {So White captures on f6. And our queen gets out. We will follow simple development from here and castle long.} 8...Qxf6 9.Bg2 {White goes for kingside development. We'll bring our bishop is out to f5, the most active square.} 9...Bf5 10.O-O {White castles.} 10...O-O-O {And we'll do the same, but on the opposite side of the board. We've got the bishop pair and a clear plan to attack on the kingside with moves such as ...g5 and ...h5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Alternatives on Move 5 / Study 2: White Plays 5.Bg5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/27ce5d3d-15c8-4356-813f-1e609ca9b9fe/studies/7c95a7e5-c5d0-4e9e-9803-6a5605cb9062/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will show us how to approach 5.Bg5 by White on move 5. Using their pawn on e5, White wants to put pressure on our queen early and provoke a trade, disrupting our development. Let's see what the antidote is. We'll begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White takes. We'll push forward with d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {Attacking the pawn. We'll develop, defending it.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg5 {And White goes for this aggressive move 9% of the time. Obviously, our queen is under attack and we can't move it to a useful square. We have to block. I recommend ...f6! We offer a trade of pawns, but we open the e- and the f-file and speed up our development. This is a sharper response than ...Nge7 and might catch White off guard.} 5...f6 6.exf6 {White captures. Let's recapture with the knight.} 6...Nxf6 7.Bxf6 {This capture is actually pretty common, played 25% of the time. White doesn't want any complications but simply gets rid of our knight. We'll recapture, activating our queen.} 7...Qxf6 8.Nbd2 {White develops.} 8...Bf5 {We have a straightforward plan. We'll castle long and push the g-pawn to attack the knight. We've got much more space than White and their setup has left their pieces stuck together. Black is definitely better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Alternatives on Move 5 / Study 2: White Plays 5.Bg5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/27ce5d3d-15c8-4356-813f-1e609ca9b9fe/studies/7c95a7e5-c5d0-4e9e-9803-6a5605cb9062/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will show us how to approach 5.Bg5 by White on move 5. Using their pawn on e5, White wants to put pressure on our queen early and provoke a trade, disrupting our development. Let's see what the antidote is. We'll begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White takes. We'll push forward with d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {Attacking the pawn. We'll develop, defending it.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg5 {And White goes for this aggressive move 9% of the time. Obviously, our queen is under attack and we can't move it to a useful square. We have to block. I recommend ...f6! We offer a trade of pawns, but we open the e- and the f-file and speed up our development. This is a sharper response than ...Nge7 and might catch White off guard.} 5...f6 6.exf6 {White captures. Let's recapture with the knight.} 6...Nxf6 7.Nbd2 {White develops the knight here 20% of the time. The knight wants to jump to the b3-square and attack our pawn. We'll grab a tempo and attack the bishop.} 7...h6 8.Bh4 {White retreating to h4 poses a question. Do we want to play ...g5 or is it too weakening for the kingside? The answer is that we do! We can use this maneuver to fianchetto on g7. Convenient.} 8...g5 9.Bg3 {White retreats to g3... And we develop the bishop. It will act as an effective defender of the d4-pawn.} 9...Bg7 10.Nb3 {White finally jumps to b3 and attacks the d4-pawn...} 10...Ne4 {But we can centralize the knight and attack the active bishop on g3. We've got three pieces defending the pawn. If White plays e3, you can capture and even accept the queen trade. The b2-pawn will be hanging. Apart from that, White is struggling to find ideas.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Alternatives on Move 5 / Study 2: White Plays 5.Bg5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/27ce5d3d-15c8-4356-813f-1e609ca9b9fe/studies/7c95a7e5-c5d0-4e9e-9803-6a5605cb9062/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will show us how to approach 5.Bg5 by White on move 5. Using their pawn on e5, White wants to put pressure on our queen early and provoke a trade, disrupting our development. Let's see what the antidote is. We'll begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White takes. We'll push forward with d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {Attacking the pawn. We'll develop, defending it.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg5 {And White goes for this aggressive move 9% of the time. Obviously, our queen is under attack and we can't move it to a useful square. We have to block. I recommend ...f6! We offer a trade of pawns, but we open the e- and the f-file and speed up our development. This is a sharper response than ...Nge7 and might catch White off guard.} 5...f6 6.exf6 {White captures. Let's recapture with the knight.} 6...Nxf6 7.e3 {White challenging our pawn now happens a quarter of the time. It's an inaccuracy for a reason we are already familiar with. White has done nothing about the b4-square. So we'll give a check there.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Nbd2 {Now the knight has to block. It is different, however, because if we take on e3 the bishop on g5 can recapture. Which is one of the benefits of White having played 5.Bg5. That's why we will attack the bishop.} 8...h6 9.Bh4 {Retreating to h4 doesn't help. We'll continue chasing the bishop.} 9...g5 10.Bg3 {White retreats to g3. And we'll now capture on e3. There is no bishop to recapture and therefore White ends up with a weak central pawn.} 10...dxe3 11.fxe3 {The f-pawn recaptures and it's now extremely weak.} 11...Bf5 {We'll simply finish development and attack the pawn with ...Qe7 next. Our king is going to castle queenside. Black is better because of the strong bishops and the clear target on e3 which we will organize our attack towards.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Alternatives on Move 5 / Study 2: White Plays 5.Bg5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/27ce5d3d-15c8-4356-813f-1e609ca9b9fe/studies/7c95a7e5-c5d0-4e9e-9803-6a5605cb9062/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will show us how to approach 5.Bg5 by White on move 5. Using their pawn on e5, White wants to put pressure on our queen early and provoke a trade, disrupting our development. Let's see what the antidote is. We'll begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White takes. We'll push forward with d4.} 3...d4 4.Nf3 {Attacking the pawn. We'll develop, defending it.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg5 {And White goes for this aggressive move 9% of the time. Obviously, our queen is under attack and we can't move it to a useful square. We have to block. I recommend ...f6! We offer a trade of pawns, but we open the e- and the f-file and speed up our development. This is a sharper response than ...Nge7 and might catch White off guard.} 5...f6 6.exf6 {White captures. Let's recapture with the knight.} 6...Nxf6 7.Nbd2 {White develops the knight here 20% of the time. The knight wants to jump to the b3-square and attack our pawn. We'll grab a tempo and attack the bishop.} 7...h6 8.Bxf6 {If White captures...} 8...Qxf6 {We can just recapture with the queen, and there are no issues in the position. The bishop is coming to f5 and we'll castle. Standard.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nc3  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/0eef3756-0d38-4db6-8c40-abeccc1c04b0/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 5 of our Albin course will show what happens when White doesn't accept the gambit pawn on move 3! This usually shows that White isn't prepared to go into the aggressive lines of the gambit and wants a calmer game. Study 1 will deal with 3.Nc3. We'll begin with White's 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.Nc3 {And now the knight comes out on move 3. It's played just over 20% of the time in almost 2 million games. White doesn't capture either pawn, but rather reinforces the attack on the d5-pawn. They might prefer capturing it with the knight. When this happens, it's usually a good idea to capture the d4-pawn. If White doesn't want to take, we will!} 3...exd4 4.Qxd4 {By far the most common move, played 88% of the time, is White recapturing on d4 with the queen. This seems logical because there is immense pressure on the d5-pawn now. In fact, we can't really defend it. What we can do is develop with tempo and gambit the pawn this way.} 4...Nc6 5.Qxd5 {Very rarely does White abstain from capturing on d5. We don't want to trade queens here and activate their knight. We can continue attacking the queen instead with Be6. For the price of a pawn, we already have two pieces developed with tempo.} 5...Be6 6.Qxd8+ {White will most likely capture here. We'll recapture with the rook and we've got a lot of potential in the position. Our knight would love to reach the c2-square from either d4 or b4. Meanwhile, the c4-pawn is under attack. Keep in mind that 7.Nf3 loses on the spot due to ...Nb4, and it's the second most popular move!} 6...Rxd8 7.Bf4 {A move that looks more active than Bd2 is Bf4. White is not going to castle yet and gives up the c4-pawn. However, the bishop is attacking c7. Let's take the pawn anyway. We'll be able to attack the white bishop and keep developing faster.} 7...Bxc4 8.Bxc7 {White captures. And it's better to keep the rook on the d-file than sliding it to c8, since we're preventing White from castling.} 8...Rd7 9.Bf4 {And when White goes back to f4... We can threaten a fork on c2!} 9...Nb4 10.Rc1 {White has to stop it with the rook. But that loses the pawn on a2.} 10...Nxa2 11.Nxa2 {White trades.} 11...Bxa2 {And we'll recapture. Although we've won back the pawn, there's a lot of chess to be played. A smart idea would be to try and give a check on b4 since White's king is completely restricted. Apart from that, our knight is coming to the natural f6-square.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nc3  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/0eef3756-0d38-4db6-8c40-abeccc1c04b0/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 5 of our Albin course will show what happens when White doesn't accept the gambit pawn on move 3! This usually shows that White isn't prepared to go into the aggressive lines of the gambit and wants a calmer game. Study 1 will deal with 3.Nc3. We'll begin with White's 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.Nc3 {And now the knight comes out on move 3. It's played just over 20% of the time in almost 2 million games. White doesn't capture either pawn, but rather reinforces the attack on the d5-pawn. They might prefer capturing it with the knight. When this happens, it's usually a good idea to capture the d4-pawn. If White doesn't want to take, we will!} 3...exd4 4.Qxd4 {By far the most common move, played 88% of the time, is White recapturing on d4 with the queen. This seems logical because there is immense pressure on the d5-pawn now. In fact, we can't really defend it. What we can do is develop with tempo and gambit the pawn this way.} 4...Nc6 5.Qxd5 {Very rarely does White abstain from capturing on d5. We don't want to trade queens here and activate their knight. We can continue attacking the queen instead with Be6. For the price of a pawn, we already have two pieces developed with tempo.} 5...Be6 6.Qxd8+ {White will most likely capture here. We'll recapture with the rook and we've got a lot of potential in the position. Our knight would love to reach the c2-square from either d4 or b4. Meanwhile, the c4-pawn is under attack. Keep in mind that 7.Nf3 loses on the spot due to ...Nb4, and it's the second most popular move!} 6...Rxd8 7.e3 {And even more surprisingly, the move that is the most popular and played a third of the time loses for the exact same reason. White defends the c4-pawn but overlooks our other threat. I expect you will win a bunch of games like this! The knight has the b4-square! White can't stop the check on c2 now.} 7...Nb4 8.Rb1 {The best they can do is move the rook so that they don't get forked. We'll give the check anyway.} 8...Nc2+ 9.Ke2 {The king has to slide to e2, and now the c4-pawn is up for grabs.} 9...Bxc4+ {And we'll take it with check. White has to move to f3 after which we will win the bishop on f1. This is completely winning for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nc3  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/0eef3756-0d38-4db6-8c40-abeccc1c04b0/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 5 of our Albin course will show what happens when White doesn't accept the gambit pawn on move 3! This usually shows that White isn't prepared to go into the aggressive lines of the gambit and wants a calmer game. Study 1 will deal with 3.Nc3. We'll begin with White's 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.Nc3 {And now the knight comes out on move 3. It's played just over 20% of the time in almost 2 million games. White doesn't capture either pawn, but rather reinforces the attack on the d5-pawn. They might prefer capturing it with the knight. When this happens, it's usually a good idea to capture the d4-pawn. If White doesn't want to take, we will!} 3...exd4 4.Qxd4 {By far the most common move, played 88% of the time, is White recapturing on d4 with the queen. This seems logical because there is immense pressure on the d5-pawn now. In fact, we can't really defend it. What we can do is develop with tempo and gambit the pawn this way.} 4...Nc6 5.Qxd5 {Very rarely does White abstain from capturing on d5. We don't want to trade queens here and activate their knight. We can continue attacking the queen instead with Be6. For the price of a pawn, we already have two pieces developed with tempo.} 5...Be6 6.Qxd8+ {White will most likely capture here. We'll recapture with the rook and we've got a lot of potential in the position. Our knight would love to reach the c2-square from either d4 or b4. Meanwhile, the c4-pawn is under attack. Keep in mind that 7.Nf3 loses on the spot due to ...Nb4, and it's the second most popular move!} 6...Rxd8 7.Bd2 {White could go for the passive-looking Bd2, with the intention of castling queenside quickly. The bishop is blocking the d-file. Let's just capture the free pawn.} 7...Bxc4 8.O-O-O {White castles...} 8...Nf6 {And we'll develop the knight to f6. We've equalized in material and have most of our pieces out. If White plays e4, you can trade the light-squared bishops. If we trade light-squared bishops on f1, we also have the plan of Nb4 and Nd3+.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nc3  / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/0eef3756-0d38-4db6-8c40-abeccc1c04b0/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 5 of our Albin course will show what happens when White doesn't accept the gambit pawn on move 3! This usually shows that White isn't prepared to go into the aggressive lines of the gambit and wants a calmer game. Study 1 will deal with 3.Nc3. We'll begin with White's 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.Nc3 {And now the knight comes out on move 3. It's played just over 20% of the time in almost 2 million games. White doesn't capture either pawn, but rather reinforces the attack on the d5-pawn. They might prefer capturing it with the knight. When this happens, it's usually a good idea to capture the d4-pawn. If White doesn't want to take, we will!} 3...exd4 4.Qxd4 {By far the most common move, played 88% of the time, is White recapturing on d4 with the queen. This seems logical because there is immense pressure on the d5-pawn now. In fact, we can't really defend it. What we can do is develop with tempo and gambit the pawn this way.} 4...Nc6 5.Qd1 {Retreating to d1 is too passive and gives us what we want.} 5...d4 {And that's to push the pawn. Now there are no problems in the position and we are simply better.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nc3  / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/0eef3756-0d38-4db6-8c40-abeccc1c04b0/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 5 of our Albin course will show what happens when White doesn't accept the gambit pawn on move 3! This usually shows that White isn't prepared to go into the aggressive lines of the gambit and wants a calmer game. Study 1 will deal with 3.Nc3. We'll begin with White's 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.Nc3 {And now the knight comes out on move 3. It's played just over 20% of the time in almost 2 million games. White doesn't capture either pawn, but rather reinforces the attack on the d5-pawn. They might prefer capturing it with the knight. When this happens, it's usually a good idea to capture the d4-pawn. If White doesn't want to take, we will!} 3...exd4 4.Nxd5 {Taking with the knight on d5 is only played 8% of the time, and for a reason. White's knight is oddly placed on d5 and will get kicked around. We'll start attacking it with ...c6. Already, there's only one square the knight can jump to.} 4...c6 5.Nf4 {And that's f4. Before we continue to chase the knight, it's important to develop. We also take away the potential h5-square from the knight this way.} 5...Nf6 6.Nf3 {White activates the other knight. Now there's two attackers on the d4-pawn. We can continue in a calmer manner and develop while defending, but I suggest a sharper plan. How should we continue? We can play ...g5! Sacrificing the pawn, we attack the knight one more time.} 6...g5 7.Nd3 {The best move for White is to retreat to d3. But we can continue chasing their pieces with g4. This is a good idea because it completely disrupts White's developing plans, while giving us space on the kingside.} 7...g4 8.Nfe5 {White jumps to the center, but the knight can only be a target there. In fact, the knight on e5 doesn't have any free squares now.} 8...Ne4 {This centralizing move is important to remember because we are threatening ...Qa5+ to trade everything off. If that happens, we will have ...f6 to trap the knight!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nc3  / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/0eef3756-0d38-4db6-8c40-abeccc1c04b0/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 5 of our Albin course will show what happens when White doesn't accept the gambit pawn on move 3! This usually shows that White isn't prepared to go into the aggressive lines of the gambit and wants a calmer game. Study 1 will deal with 3.Nc3. We'll begin with White's 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.Nc3 {And now the knight comes out on move 3. It's played just over 20% of the time in almost 2 million games. White doesn't capture either pawn, but rather reinforces the attack on the d5-pawn. They might prefer capturing it with the knight. When this happens, it's usually a good idea to capture the d4-pawn. If White doesn't want to take, we will!} 3...exd4 4.Nxd5 {Taking with the knight on d5 is only played 8% of the time, and for a reason. White's knight is oddly placed on d5 and will get kicked around. We'll start attacking it with ...c6. Already, there's only one square the knight can jump to.} 4...c6 5.Nf4 {And that's f4. Before we continue to chase the knight, it's important to develop. We also take away the potential h5-square from the knight this way.} 5...Nf6 6.Nf3 {White activates the other knight. Now there's two attackers on the d4-pawn. We can continue in a calmer manner and develop while defending, but I suggest a sharper plan. How should we continue? We can play ...g5! Sacrificing the pawn, we attack the knight one more time.} 6...g5 7.Nxg5 {Capturing on g5 loses to a tactic.} 7...Qa5+ {We have a fork on a5 and we will win the knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: The Albin Declined (3.e3) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/fafc6dcc-5b7f-4837-921a-56aa8cc04ef4/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will focus on 3.e3 by White. It is a passive move, but could lead to a solid setup by White and normal development by both sides without much confrontation early. That is what White wants when they play e3, but it also shows the flexibility of the Albin that Black can get a good position even without fireworks on the board! We'll begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll go for the Albin.} 2...e5 3.e3 {It definitely seems like White is scared when they play this move. It is usually suboptimal to defend this early when you're facing a gambit. The move 3.e3 is played 13% of the time in more than 1.2 million games. Let's take on d4.} 3...exd4 4.Qxd4 {The queen recapturing will get White another pawn, but will give us a lot of freedom and activity. It's played around 20% of the time. We'll attack the queen first, gambiting the d-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Qxd5 {Once White captures on d5, I suggest avoiding the trade, although that is playable as well. I think it's better to slide the queen to e7. We want to leave the White queen on d5 and make it a target with moves like ...Nf6 and ...Nb4. Also, our light-squared bishop is coming to either e6 or f5 to help out.} 5...Qe7 6.Nf3 {Let's demonstrate how we can extract the most out of the position. We will both attack the queen and threaten a fork on c2.} 6...Nb4 7.Qd1 {This forces the queen back into passivity. Let's renew the threat of the fork by playing Bf5.} 7...Bf5 8.Na3 {While we are developing in the optimal way, White has to defend with moves like Na3.} 8...Rd8 {We can continue improving while grabbing tempi with ...Rd8 here, attacking the queen. White will have to move the queen again and we can continue with logical developing moves like ...Nf6, as well as activating the dark-squared bishop. We can also contemplate ...Nd3+ in some variations to get White's light-squared bishop. ...c6 is also good to defend b7.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: The Albin Declined (3.e3) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/fafc6dcc-5b7f-4837-921a-56aa8cc04ef4/lines"]

1.d4 {Study 2 will focus on 3.e3 by White. It is a passive move, but could lead to a solid setup by White and normal development by both sides without much confrontation early. That is what White wants when they play e3, but it also shows the flexibility of the Albin that Black can get a good position even without fireworks on the board! We'll begin with 1.d4 d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. We'll go for the Albin.} 2...e5 3.e3 {It definitely seems like White is scared when they play this move. It is usually suboptimal to defend this early when you're facing a gambit. The move 3.e3 is played 13% of the time in more than 1.2 million games. Let's take on d4.} 3...exd4 4.exd4 {White will recapture with the pawn almost three quarters of the time. It makes sense to keep the pawns in the center. This line will be a lot more peaceful than the usual Albin variations, but that's okay. Every gambit has a positional side, too. When there are no tricks or potential for immediate aggression, it's important to develop optimally.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White's knight on c3 does a good job controlling the center and especially the d5-square. Which is why we will pin it. Not necessarily to capture it, just to restrict it.} 5...Bb4 6.Nf3 {White continues development... And we'll castle, focusing on queenside development next.} 6...O-O 7.Be2 {White will probably play Be2 rather than capture on d5. In that case, we can take on c4 and make them move the bishop again! We gain a tempo this way.} 7...dxc4 8.Bxc4 {White captures. They now have an IQP on d4 which is automatically our primary target. How will we approach it? Well, we will pin its defender first.} 8...Bg4 9.O-O {White castles... And now we'll bring the knight to c6, with the concept of taking on f3 and then d4. We've activated both queenside pieces in the last two moves and already managed to create a threat on the isolated pawn.} 9...Nc6 10.Be3 {White defends with the bishop for now.} 10...Qd6 {But we aren't done yet. A good plan now is to bring the rook to the d-file to support the queen. Also, rather than capturing the c3-knight, the bishop would be much more useful on b6 from where it would attack the pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: White Takes The Other Pawn (3.cxd5) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/393900c2-1eb1-4d91-a56e-5088859ef62e/lines"]

1.d4 {We've mostly considered White taking the pawn on e5 after the Albin, as that is the offered pawn. But what if White takes the pawn on d5 instead? This leads into a whole new world of theory which we will explore in the final study of this chapter. We'll begin with 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.cxd5 {White captures on d5 13% of the time, in almost 1.2 million games. Their idea is to leave the tension between the pawns on e5 and d4 but attract our queen to the center, so that they can get some developing moves with tempo before they take on e5. We will still recapture with the queen, attacking the d4-pawn. White has a lot of options here.} 3...Qxd5 4.Nc3 {The most common move is Nc3 here, attacking the queen, and it's played a third of the time. However, White gives up the d4-pawn! We will gladly capture. Let's see their idea.} 4...Qxd4 5.Qxd4 {Firstly, they want to trade the queens. And after we recapture...} 5...exd4 6.Nb5 {The knight comes to b5 and threatens both a fork as well as the d4-pawn. Unfortunately, we can't defend both. We will defend against the greater threat on c7, of course.} 6...Na6 7.Nxd4 {White captures in the center now. But we can develop with tempo as well. Why not give a check on b4 when the knight has ventured away?} 7...Bb4+ 8.Bd2 {The only way White can block now is with the bishop on d2. A trade is fine for us.} 8...Bxd2+ 9.Kxd2 {White recaptures with the king. Losing castling rights isn't as much of a factor here, because the queens have been traded. Let's continue expanding, while attacking White's misplaced knight.} 9...c5 10.Nb5 {The knight can jump back to b5...} 10...Be6 {But we are well protected and there's easy development to come. We don't even have to castle. Placing the knight on f6 and the king on e7 is safe enough. Our rooks will probably come to the center quicker than White's.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: White Takes The Other Pawn (3.cxd5) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/393900c2-1eb1-4d91-a56e-5088859ef62e/lines"]

1.d4 {We've mostly considered White taking the pawn on e5 after the Albin, as that is the offered pawn. But what if White takes the pawn on d5 instead? This leads into a whole new world of theory which we will explore in the final study of this chapter. We'll begin with 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.cxd5 {White captures on d5 13% of the time, in almost 1.2 million games. Their idea is to leave the tension between the pawns on e5 and d4 but attract our queen to the center, so that they can get some developing moves with tempo before they take on e5. We will still recapture with the queen, attacking the d4-pawn. White has a lot of options here.} 3...Qxd5 4.Nf3 {A move that's played 27% of the time is Nf3. It feels the most natural to protect the center before attacking our queen with Nc3. The e5-pawn is also under pressure. So we don't have many options but to push and attack the knight back.} 4...e4 5.Nc3 {White has an intermezzo with Nc3. It would be great for them if we just passively moved the queen away. But we have the Bb4 pin, which is also the best move.} 5...Bb4 6.Qa4+ {White could force our hand and go for the check on a4 immediately, trying to exploit the position of our bishop on b4. But we are the only ones who have the potential to come out on top in this line. We'll block with the knight and defend the bishop.} 6...Nc6 7.Qxb4 {Now White has a cunning continuation where they actually capture the bishop. Now our queen is under attack, since the pin has been eliminated. But this is just a fancy way to trade pieces, and we capture the queen.} 7...Nxb4 8.Nxd5 {White captures our queen. White is threatening a fork as well as us, so it doesn't pay off to jump into c2. We'll get the piece back instead. The knight is still under attack on f3.} 8...Nxd5 9.Ne5 {White jumps to the center. Before we develop, we could improve one of our pieces and reintroduce a threat. Nb4 is useful because White has to react to the threat of Nc2+ again.} 9...Nb4 10.Kd1 {And with the king, too! Now it's finally time to continue development.} 10...Be6 {It's important to activate the bishop to e6 first. This is designed so that we have ...Bb3+ if White plays a3, which would give us a big advantage with ...Nc2 coming after it. Apart from that, ...f6 is good to kick away White's only active piece and our flexible knight can develop to e7.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: White Takes The Other Pawn (3.cxd5) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/393900c2-1eb1-4d91-a56e-5088859ef62e/lines"]

1.d4 {We've mostly considered White taking the pawn on e5 after the Albin, as that is the offered pawn. But what if White takes the pawn on d5 instead? This leads into a whole new world of theory which we will explore in the final study of this chapter. We'll begin with 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.cxd5 {White captures on d5 13% of the time, in almost 1.2 million games. Their idea is to leave the tension between the pawns on e5 and d4 but attract our queen to the center, so that they can get some developing moves with tempo before they take on e5. We will still recapture with the queen, attacking the d4-pawn. White has a lot of options here.} 3...Qxd5 4.e3 {White chooses to protect the center with a pawn over 20% of the time. Let's isolate their pawn by capturing on d4.} 4...exd4 5.exd4 {And we have created an IQP with this trade, which could prove to be a downside for White. In any case, it makes our position easy to play. We will just harass the pawn as much as possible, starting by developing with Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Nf3 {White defends. You probably already know the next move. It's pinning the knight on f3.} 6...Bg4 7.Be2 {White defends. Don't rush and capture on f3 and then on d4. That would lose the queen after Bxc6+! We can give a check on b4 instead.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Nc3 {White blocks with the knight, which is also pinned now. Our idea was to attract the knight to c3 and only then capture on f3. Why?} 8...Bxf3 9.Bxf3 {Well, once the bishop recaptures we can place the queen on c4 and prevent castling! This could be dangerous for White, especially if they let us castle and bring the rook to the e-file. Also, the knight on c3 is under attack twice.} 9...Qc4 10.Bxc6+ {White captures with check first. We'll recapture with the pawn to keep the queen on c4.} 10...bxc6 11.Qe2+ {The best way for White to get out of trouble is force a queen trade. We have to take.} 11...Qxe2+ 12.Kxe2 {The king recaptures.} 12...Ne7 {But we keep the same ideas. The pawn on d4 is still isolated and weak and we'll target it with ...Nf5. We will still castle long and our rooks are coming to the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: White Takes The Other Pawn (3.cxd5) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/393900c2-1eb1-4d91-a56e-5088859ef62e/lines"]

1.d4 {We've mostly considered White taking the pawn on e5 after the Albin, as that is the offered pawn. But what if White takes the pawn on d5 instead? This leads into a whole new world of theory which we will explore in the final study of this chapter. We'll begin with 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.cxd5 {White captures on d5 13% of the time, in almost 1.2 million games. Their idea is to leave the tension between the pawns on e5 and d4 but attract our queen to the center, so that they can get some developing moves with tempo before they take on e5. We will still recapture with the queen, attacking the d4-pawn. White has a lot of options here.} 3...Qxd5 4.dxe5 {Taking on e5 now doesn't make a whole lot of sense. It's played just under 20% of the time. We'll accept the queen trade and ensure White loses castling rights. White has not played the opening very positively.} 4...Qxd1+ 5.Kxd1 {White recaptures. And now we can attack the pawn with Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Nf3 {White defends, but we already know the common Albin ideas that we can implement here as well.} 6...Nge7 {We can bring the knight to e7 from where it will jump to g6. Our bishop is coming to either of the three squares that are e6, f5 or g4, and we'll castle long. We already have a nice position and jare likely to win the pawn on e5 back.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: White Takes The Other Pawn (3.cxd5) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/393900c2-1eb1-4d91-a56e-5088859ef62e/lines"]

1.d4 {We've mostly considered White taking the pawn on e5 after the Albin, as that is the offered pawn. But what if White takes the pawn on d5 instead? This leads into a whole new world of theory which we will explore in the final study of this chapter. We'll begin with 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.cxd5 {White captures on d5 13% of the time, in almost 1.2 million games. Their idea is to leave the tension between the pawns on e5 and d4 but attract our queen to the center, so that they can get some developing moves with tempo before they take on e5. We will still recapture with the queen, attacking the d4-pawn. White has a lot of options here.} 3...Qxd5 4.e3 {White chooses to protect the center with a pawn over 20% of the time. Let's isolate their pawn by capturing on d4.} 4...exd4 5.Qxd4 {If the queen recaptures, our ideas of harassing the IQP will stay the same, just without the queens. We'll accept the trade.} 5...Qxd4 6.exd4 {White takes, and now the pawn is isolated again. Let's attack it immediately whilst also developing.} 6...Nc6 7.Nf3 {White defends with the knight.} 7...Bg4 {But we will attack it with our bishop. There are also ideas of ...Nb4 to threaten the fork, and we will castle queenside, of course. The pawn on d4 is still vulnerable.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: White Takes The Other Pawn (3.cxd5) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/393900c2-1eb1-4d91-a56e-5088859ef62e/lines"]

1.d4 {We've mostly considered White taking the pawn on e5 after the Albin, as that is the offered pawn. But what if White takes the pawn on d5 instead? This leads into a whole new world of theory which we will explore in the final study of this chapter. We'll begin with 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.cxd5 {White captures on d5 13% of the time, in almost 1.2 million games. Their idea is to leave the tension between the pawns on e5 and d4 but attract our queen to the center, so that they can get some developing moves with tempo before they take on e5. We will still recapture with the queen, attacking the d4-pawn. White has a lot of options here.} 3...Qxd5 4.Nf3 {A move that's played 27% of the time is Nf3. It feels the most natural to protect the center before attacking our queen with Nc3. The e5-pawn is also under pressure. So we don't have many options but to push and attack the knight back.} 4...e4 5.Nc3 {White has an intermezzo with Nc3. It would be great for them if we just passively moved the queen away. But we have the Bb4 pin, which is also the best move.} 5...Bb4 6.Nd2 {A much safer move than Ne5 is Nd2. It turns out that White didn't have many options for the knight after all. This gives us the d4-pawn but attacks the e4-pawn.} 6...Qxd4 7.e3 {First of all, White can use the chance to play e3 with tempo, attacking our queen. I recommend the d6-square, even though our queen will be attacked again on the next move. We will transfer it to the kingside.} 7...Qd6 8.Ncxe4 {White does capture on e4.} 8...Qg6 {And our queen is safe and strong on g6, actually restricting White's bishop from developing comfortably. Also, the knight on e4 is currently under attack because its defender is pinned. Our knights will come out next, with a lively position to come.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: White Takes The Other Pawn (3.cxd5) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/393900c2-1eb1-4d91-a56e-5088859ef62e/lines"]

1.d4 {We've mostly considered White taking the pawn on e5 after the Albin, as that is the offered pawn. But what if White takes the pawn on d5 instead? This leads into a whole new world of theory which we will explore in the final study of this chapter. We'll begin with 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.cxd5 {White captures on d5 13% of the time, in almost 1.2 million games. Their idea is to leave the tension between the pawns on e5 and d4 but attract our queen to the center, so that they can get some developing moves with tempo before they take on e5. We will still recapture with the queen, attacking the d4-pawn. White has a lot of options here.} 3...Qxd5 4.Nf3 {A move that's played 27% of the time is Nf3. It feels the most natural to protect the center before attacking our queen with Nc3. The e5-pawn is also under pressure. So we don't have many options but to push and attack the knight back.} 4...e4 5.Nc3 {White has an intermezzo with Nc3. It would be great for them if we just passively moved the queen away. But we have the Bb4 pin, which is also the best move.} 5...Bb4 6.Ne5 {White could just blitz out Ne5 here, seeing that their knight is under attack and thinking that e5 is the most active square. In fact, this is the most common move. But it's a mistake. Let's capture on c3 first to neutralize any Qa4+ ideas.} 6...Bxc3+ 7.bxc3 {Why was Ne5 a mistake? Well, after f6 the knight is trapped!} 7...f6 8.c4 {White's best effort to keep fighting is c4, attacking the queen. But we can simply slide to e6. As long as the knight is still trapped, we're good.} 8...Qe6 9.Qa4+ {White can try the check on a4.} 9...c6 {But they won't be saving the knight after ...c6. We will be winning a piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Early Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: White Takes The Other Pawn (3.cxd5) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1a186efb-82c1-4d71-82de-874f3644c474/chapters/82e78330-45ec-4064-ba1f-caceb7eb44ff/studies/393900c2-1eb1-4d91-a56e-5088859ef62e/lines"]

1.d4 {We've mostly considered White taking the pawn on e5 after the Albin, as that is the offered pawn. But what if White takes the pawn on d5 instead? This leads into a whole new world of theory which we will explore in the final study of this chapter. We'll begin with 1.d4 and our reply of 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit. Let's play the Albin.} 2...e5 3.cxd5 {White captures on d5 13% of the time, in almost 1.2 million games. Their idea is to leave the tension between the pawns on e5 and d4 but attract our queen to the center, so that they can get some developing moves with tempo before they take on e5. We will still recapture with the queen, attacking the d4-pawn. White has a lot of options here.} 3...Qxd5 4.Nf3 {A move that's played 27% of the time is Nf3. It feels the most natural to protect the center before attacking our queen with Nc3. The e5-pawn is also under pressure. So we don't have many options but to push and attack the knight back.} 4...e4 5.Nc3 {White has an intermezzo with Nc3. It would be great for them if we just passively moved the queen away. But we have the Bb4 pin, which is also the best move.} 5...Bb4 6.Ne5 {White could just blitz out Ne5 here, seeing that their knight is under attack and thinking that e5 is the most active square. In fact, this is the most common move. But it's a mistake. Let's capture on c3 first to neutralize any Qa4+ ideas.} 6...Bxc3+ 7.bxc3 {Why was Ne5 a mistake? Well, after f6 the knight is trapped!} 7...f6 8.Qa4+ {If White gives this check now, it's even worse.} 8...b5 {We'll just block with the pawn and now the queen is attacked as well. This is completely winning for Black.} *